{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1555429-black-vans-a-storyteller-s-guide-to-deviant-the-renegades","id":1555429,"title":"Black Vans - A Storyteller's Guide to Deviant: The Renegades","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Deviant: The Renegades"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-11-13T04:07:42-08:00","id":1555429,"title":"Black Vans - A Storyteller's Guide to Deviant: The Renegades","content":"You thought you escaped. You thought you were free. After six months without hearing anything from them, you thought for sure that they had forgotten about you. But then you spot a black van in the street near the place you\u2019ve been squatting, and your heart pounds. Your adrenaline, and your rage, surge. You remember all the terror, all the blood, all the death. You haven\u2019t needed to dig shallow graves in close to a year and a half, but that black van brings back all the awful memories.<br /> <br /> But it\u2019s just one, ordinary-looking black van, right?<br /> <br /> Then you spot another across the street from the coffee shop where you work. And another a block away from a friend\u2019s house. And yet another near the park where you go for your morning jog. After a day or two, you can no longer deny it: They\u2019ve found you, and soon the conspiracy\u2019s agents will come.<br /> <br /> In case you don't know me, I was the co-developer of <b>Deviant: The Renegades</b> and the line developer for it after that. Because the IP owner stopped approving new <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> projects from Onyx Path Publishing, I have decided to continue the <b>Deviant</b> line on the Storyteller's Vault. I released <b>Shallow Graves</b> in July 2024, and my second offering, which is currently in 1st proofs, is <b>Black Vans \u2013 A Storyteller\u2019s Guide</b>. I anticipate a full release in early 2026, and in anticipation of that, I will be sharing excerpts of the text a few times a week until release day \u2013 starting this Saturday.<br /> <br /> Today is the cover reveal to whet your appetites.<br /> <br /> <img data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/143487653/dc89b717c75441ccb6b2dda41a4a8659/eyJ3IjoxMDgwfQ%3D%3D/1.png?token-hash=-SytDwkLZn1jH7w2t6PLiccrTYz49ml0F3KqGbwx2VE%3D&amp;token-time=1764288000\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/143487653/dc89b717c75441ccb6b2dda41a4a8659/eyJ3IjoxMDgwfQ%3D%3D/1.png?token-hash=-SytDwkLZn1jH7w2t6PLiccrTYz49ml0F3KqGbwx2VE%3D&amp;token-time=1764288000\" data-thumb-url=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/143487653/dc89b717c75441ccb6b2dda41a4a8659/eyJ3IjoxMDgwfQ%3D%3D/1.png?token-hash=-SytDwkLZn1jH7w2t6PLiccrTYz49ml0F3KqGbwx2VE%3D&amp;token-time=1764288000\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/><br /> <img data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/143487653/17159648de5b48bebbf72af9d696c71e/eyJ3IjoxMDgwfQ%3D%3D/1.jpg?token-hash=Z7f71qdCpfC2N1Um06qXjszDB3zswkJZhawgwImOGio%3D&amp;token-time=1764288000\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/143487653/17159648de5b48bebbf72af9d696c71e/eyJ3IjoxMDgwfQ%3D%3D/1.jpg?token-hash=Z7f71qdCpfC2N1Um06qXjszDB3zswkJZhawgwImOGio%3D&amp;token-time=1764288000\" data-thumb-url=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/143487653/17159648de5b48bebbf72af9d696c71e/eyJ3IjoxMDgwfQ%3D%3D/1.jpg?token-hash=Z7f71qdCpfC2N1Um06qXjszDB3zswkJZhawgwImOGio%3D&amp;token-time=1764288000\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":4},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-14T00:58:32-08:00","id":1555466,"title":"","content":"This looks like it's going to be great!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-15T04:09:38-08:00","id":1555489,"title":"","content":"Today I'm sharing a bit of the introduction \u2013 a summary of its contents, really. <b>Shallow Graves</b> and <b>Black Vans</b> were the product of about 18 months of work apiece, but I've had their seeds in my pocket since before I turned in <b>The Clades Companion</b> and <b>The Devoted Companion</b> for editing way back in what we can cheerlessly describe as the During Times.<br /> <br /> I had a very long time to think about what I would want to include in a Deviant player's guide and in a Deviant Storyteller's guide, and the only question in my mind before CofD approvals stopped was how many words I'd be allowed to include. As it turned out, both projects went from a &quot;propose and hopefully get approved' situation to a DIY one. I expected that to be stressful, but it turned out to be liberating. <b>Shallow Graves</b> is exactly the player's guide I envisioned for <b>Deviant</b>, and I feel the same way about how <b>Black Vans</b> turned out as a Storyteller's guide.<br /> <br /> I have, I hoped, taken some of the greatest hits of ST-facing CofD supplements (1E and 2E) and drawn them together into a single, 185,000-word book (for context, a typical CofD core is 200k-215k words). The random story hook characters of <b>Damnation City</b> and <b>Tome of the Pentacle</b>? Yes. The cross-genre play of <b>Mirrors</b>, <b>The Mage Chronicler's Guide</b>, and <b>The Demon Storyteller's Guide</b>? We're gonna do some of that. The crossover options of <b>Dark Eras</b> and <b>The Contagion Chronicle</b>? Yes, please. The optional mechanics of <b>Mirrors</b> and <b>Hurt Locker</b>? LFG.<br /> <br /> I've also thrown in a bunch of advice that is broadly applicable across much of the TTRPG hobby, because I've always felt that we tend to assume everyone picking up a Storyteller's Guide knows how to plan and run a roleplaying game, and maybe that does a disservice to folks who are just starting out.<br /> <br /> The summary below reflects that peculiar and eclectic pedigree. If you want to see more, I've also posted the first part of the chapter fiction at <a href=\"http://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">my Patreon</a>, which you can read for free (and patrons have access to the full text of the novella, if you're so inclined).<br /> <br /> How to Use This Book<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:#00000a\">This supplement for <b>Deviant: The Renegades</b> covers a wide range of topics. It requires the core <b>Deviant</b> book, and we strongly recommend access to its other supplements: <b>The Clades Companion</b>, <b>The Devoted Companion</b>, and <b>Shallow Graves</b>. The contents of this book are as follows:</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:#00000a\"><b>Introduction: Driver</b> discusses chronicle design and narrative techniques in the broadest terms \u2014 from the logistics of venue and troupe make-up to different approaches to chronicle and chapter preparation and execution.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:#00000a\"><b>Chapter One: Manual</b> digs deep into the Storytelling system\u2019s inner workings and how to get the most use out of it as a dramatic engine. It also explores <b>Deviant</b>-specific systems and offers ways to modify the game\u2019s mechanics to make them conform more closely to the Storyteller\u2019s style and the troupe\u2019s preferences.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:#00000a\"><b>Chapter Two: Engine</b> looks at <b>Deviant</b>\u2019s narrative systems and discusses the ways a Storyteller can use everything from character sheets to Touchstones to conspiracy systems to drive the chronicle\u2019s stories.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:#00000a\"><b>Chapter Three: Passengers</b> offers a tool for quickly creating Baseline and Deviant characters who can be dropped into the story at a moment\u2019s notice.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:#00000a\"><b>Chapter Four: Models</b> looks at parts of the <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> that <b>Deviant</b> has avoided addressing head-on in its core and first three supplements. It offers ways of including demons, mages, vampires, and other occult creatures (and Remade derived from them) in a <b>Deviant</b> chronicle \u2014 whether as a Storyteller character or a player\u2019s character.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"color:#00000a\"><b>Chapter Five: After-Market Accessories</b> takes <b>Deviant</b> out of its familiar genre assumptions and default gameplay loop and places the Broken in exotic settings. It also offers examples of ways to alter <b>Deviant</b> systems like Variations, Scars, Instability, and conspiracies to fit into new genres, and includes new Merits, Forms, and other mechanics appropriate to those genres.</span></span><br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-15T11:05:55-08:00","id":1555490,"title":"","content":"My question is a dry, but important one, because I really intend on buying this on my first opportunity: how much?<br /> <br /> How much is <b>Shallow Graves' </b>price and how much will this one be?<br /> <br /> Is there any places that make it easier on foreigners to buy it? Because I only have Brazilian Reais. Do you take PIX?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-15T11:38:42-08:00","id":1555491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1555429-black-vans-a-storyteller-s-guide-to-deviant-the-renegades?p=1555490#post1555490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My question is a dry, but important one, because I really intend on buying this on my first opportunity: how much?<br /> <br /> How much is <b>Shallow Graves' </b>price and how much will this one be?<br /> <br /> Is there any places that make it easier on foreigners to buy it? Because I only have Brazilian Reais. Do you take PIX?</div> </div> </div> </div>Shallow Graves <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/en/product/486079/shallow-graves\" target=\"_blank\">is available on Storytellers Vault</a> at $19.99. If you've bought something off of DriveThruRPG, then you'll be able to use the same account and payment type here. If not, they take credit and debit cards or Paypal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-18T04:15:25-08:00","id":1555594,"title":"","content":"Black Vans' introduction is no two-page handshake. Instead, it's a short chapter in itself that offers advice for planning, organizing, and running a <b>Deviant: The Renegades</b> chronicle starting with the basics \u2013 the considerations of venue, troupe size, and chronicle length. This includes suggestions for external tools to use between gaming sessions to enhance play, which is today's excerpt.<br /> <br /> At the <a href=\"http://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a>, I've posted another free excerpt about how to use knowledge of your personality and that of your players to decide how best to approach planning and running a chronicle that maximizes your advantages while compensating for your weaknesses (and patrons can read the whole introduction).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Externalities</span><br /> <br /> Not all events in a chronicle need take place during regular gaming sessions or focus solely on the players' characters. In addition to the optional vignette (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 230) and flashback (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 167) rules meant to flesh out the Deviants' backstories and antagonists at the table, troupes (or individual players) can use any or all the following.<br /> <br /> <b>Diaries:</b> This could be a diary kept in-character by one of the Remade or just his player's reflections on the Broken's experiences, theories, and lessons learned. It can be shared with the entire troupe, only the Storyteller, or with a select few trusted friends (or Touchstones). The discovery or theft of the diary can even serve as a plot point within the chronicle.<br /> <br /> <b>Epistles:</b> One of the Deviants sends letters or reports to a party outside of the cohort to brief them on the cohort's activities. Although similar to an in-character diary, epistles imply a relationship with their recipient. Who are they? Do they write letters back? What happens if someone intercepts the character's missives?<br /> <br /> <b>Bluebooking:</b> In eras past, some roleplayers would buy bluebooks \u2014 small, inexpensive notebooks of the sort used for essay questions on college exams \u2014 and pass them back and forth between gaming sessions, each one adding a bit more to a scene involving two or three characters engaged in an in-depth conversation or similar interaction. Contemporary bluebooking is more likely to take the form of email or forum threads \u2014 or as a series of DMs, in real-time digital play. Bluebooking is a great way to explore intra-cohort relationships (or relationships with significant Storyteller characters) without using up a lot of time at the gaming table. It lets the troupe focus on following plot threads or addressing other problems that involve the entire cohort, while still giving individual players an optional way of getting to know one another's characters.<br /> <br /> <b>Co-Storytellers:</b> Most roleplaying games assume a single Storyteller is responsible for planning and running a chronicle. This need not be the case, however. A Storyteller who excels at tightly plotted stories can benefit from a friend who digs designing challenging antagonists or who can feed other ideas into the mix. Internet communities have long served as sounding boards for Storytellers looking for advice or ideas, but co-Storytelling is a more formal arrangement \u2014 one that can allow the participation of someone who lives too far away to join an offline or real-time digital chronicle. For this to work, it's important that the co-Storyteller(s) have a way to stay current on chronicle events \u2014 whether through plot summaries, text logs, or recordings \u2014 and that they be in regular contact with the primary Storyteller so that they can pitch ideas back and forth.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-18T16:48:08-08:00","id":1555614,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>marin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1555429-black-vans-a-storyteller-s-guide-to-deviant-the-renegades?p=1555491#post1555491\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Shallow Graves <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/en/product/486079/shallow-graves\" target=\"_blank\">is available on Storytellers Vault</a> at $19.99. If you've bought something off of DriveThruRPG, then you'll be able to use the same account and payment type here. If not, they take credit and debit cards or Paypal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Unfortunately if that's all, it's the same as saying there's nothing to ease things for foreigners, just the same headache as always when working with dollars and backwaters payment systems...<br /> <br /> Oh, well, I'll buy it anyway when I can.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-20T04:02:01-08:00","id":1555679,"title":"","content":"Chapter One unpacks game systems, starting with the core Storytelling System. Some of this concerns existing systems, such as the Investigation rules (which you can read for free at my Patreon). Other parts offer advice for introducing new mechanics, such as the sample rules for training and commanding animals and Manticores, which I'm sharing below. Patrons also have access to the full text of the &quot;mundane&quot; half of the chapter.<br /> <br /> This chapter lays the foundation for a lot of what I end up doing in Chapters Four and Five. When I was a kid, I had an English teacher who constantly told us that it was important to know the rules so that we knew when to break them. Chapter One provides some examples of ways to break (or at least modify) the rules, but it also spends a lot of time talking about the rules as they are. When we get to the last two chapters, though, you're going to see some wild stuff that redefines important game mechanics. I can't wait to share some of it!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:22px\">Training Animals and Manticores</span><br /> <br /> <b>The Clades Companion</b> (p. 19) divides animals into three broad categories of intelligence: dull, smart, and near-sapient.<br /> <br /> Dull animals can't be trained, as they are creatures of raw instinct incapable of processing human commands. They include reptiles, fish, and invertebrates, as well as many birds.<br /> <br /> Smart animals can learn and so can be trained. Most mammals are smart, as are some birds.<br /> <br /> Near-sapient animals are exceptionally intelligent and capable of solving problems and expressing preferences. Few animals are near-sapient \u2014 dolphins, great apes, and a handful of others.<br /> <br /> A Manticore can be much more intelligent than other members of its species.<br /> <br /> Humans generally regard themselves as the only truly sapient animals on Earth. Manticores granted full sapience are Deviants with the Uplift Form (<b>Clades Companion</b>, p. 16).<br /> <br /> Training an animal \u2014 even one from a thoroughly domesticated species \u2014 takes a lot of time. Working dogs, for example, require one to two years of training before they're ready to be service animals, assuming their training starts when they're young enough and that they receive consistent training. Wild animals or ones who are less well suited to training take longer and are sometimes impossible to train.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Basic Commands</span><br /> <br /> A character with the Manticore Companion Merit (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 101) that reflects a smart or near-sapient companion, or one who has a smart animal as a pet, is assumed to have previously provided rudimentary training so that it knows how to obey the following commands when she issues them:<br /> <br /> <b>Attack:</b> The animal attacks a target indicated by its handler until it suffers bashing damage in excess of its Stamina, any amount of lethal damage, or until commanded to do otherwise.<br /> <br /> <b>Come/Heel:</b> The animal comes to its handler and follows her until commanded otherwise.<br /> <br /> <b>Down:</b> The animal stops making noise and sits or lays down (handler's choice) until commanded otherwise.<br /> <br /> <b>Leave It:</b> The animal drops whatever it is carrying or stops interacting with something it was handling.<br /> <br /> <b>Stay:</b> The animal retains its current position until commanded otherwise.<br /> <br /> Training a smart animal to obey these commands can occur during downtime lasting at least six weeks, as long as the character devotes at least an hour a day to the training. This is shortened to four weeks if the character devotes three hours a day to the training and lengthened to eight weeks if training is irregular.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Advanced Commands</span><br /> <br /> In addition to basic commands, domesticated smart or near-sapient animals can learn to obey more complex commands. Teaching a smart animal to obey a new command requires a month of practice. Smart animals can learn a number of advanced commands equal to the handler's Animal Ken dots +1, while near-sapient animals can learn three times as many commands. Wild animals can't be issued advanced commands; they only behave in these ways when they choose to.<br /> <br /> <b>Fetch:</b> The animal will fetch an object the handler throws or indicates within the scene and bring it back to her. As a separate command with Fetch as its prerequisite, the animal can be ordered to bring the object to a place or person in the scene that the handler indicates. As a separate command with Fetch as its prerequisite, the animal can be ordered to go find an object, animal, or person familiar to it (or one it identifies through Recognize) and bring it to its handler (or to someone else it recognizes whom the handler indicates).<br /> <br /> <b>Frenzy:</b> Once given the order to attack, the animal will continue to do so even if injured \u2014 until it's either incapacitated or ordered to stop.<br /> <br /> <b>Guard:</b> The animal will protect a person or place indicated by its handler. If it's approached by an unfamiliar animal, stranger, or other intruder, the animal will attempt to alert its handler. As a separate command with Guard as its prerequisite, the animal will also execute another order it knows (such as to attack) if the intruder persists \u2014 chosen when its handler gives it the order to guard.<br /> <br /> <b>Herd:</b> The animal will attempt to control the movements of a target or class of targets its handler indicates \u2014 such as keeping a child or flock of sheep away from danger or driving a bear into a trap.<br /> <br /> <b>Pack:</b> The animal will obey orders issued by one of its handler's companions (other members of the cohort, family members, etc.), although the handler's commands take precedence, and the animal must be familiar with these people before it will take orders from them. As a separate command with Pack as its prerequisite, the animal will obey orders issued to it by anyone who gives an appropriate signal (such as a hand sign or command word), although the commands of its handler and her companions take precedence.<br /> <br /> <b>Perform:</b> The animal will respond to various commands to take physical actions, such as to beg, roll over, or &quot;speak&quot;. As a separate command with Perform as its prerequisite, the animal can be taught to execute a complex series of actions, such as navigating an obstacle course.<br /> <br /> <b>Recognize:</b> The animal can be ordered to identify and interact with objects it has good familiarity with, although it can get confused by objects that resemble each other. This recognition is imprecise and reliant on the animal's senses. A colorblind animal can't be ordered to identify something as green, for example. As an additional command with Recognize as its prerequisite, the animal can recognize objects its handler describes, although the description must still be in terms the animal's senses can discern.<br /> <br /> <b>Respond:</b> The animal can be ordered to respond to certain situations, executing its other orders whenever circumstances demand. The handler's player can issue orders even when her character could not do so, but the command roll suffers a \u20134 penalty. As an additional command with Respond as its prerequisite, the penalty is only \u20132.<br /> <br /> <b>Variation:</b> The Manticore activates or deactivates a Toggled Variation or directs a Directed Variation as its handler indicates.<br /> <br /> Advanced commands don't come naturally to most animals and require regular practice. For every month that goes by during which the animal doesn't practice its advanced commands for at least an hour a day, its handler suffers a cumulative \u20131 penalty to issue these commands (to a maximum of \u20135). This penalty fades at the rate of \u20131 per month once practice resumes and can be reduced by \u20131 per week with a regimen of at least three hours per day.<br /> <br /> Near-sapient animals require more attention than merely smart ones. Insufficiently stimulated, these creatures become bored and surly. Keeping them cooperative requires at least three hours per day, and penalties for not practicing advanced commands accumulate at the rate of \u20131 per week (maximum \u20135). However, penalties fade at the rate of \u20131 per week once the animal is receiving adequate attention.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Issuing Commands</span><br /> <br /> Issuing a command the animal knows when it's in non-stressful situations usually succeeds automatically. In action scenes or other unfamiliar environments, issuing a command requires a successful Presence + Animal Ken action.<br /> <br /> <i>Suggested Modifiers: Animal is hungry (\u20131) or injured (\u20132). Command is in keeping with animal's natural instincts (+1 or +2) or in defiance of them (\u20131 to \u20133).</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Training an AI</span><br /> <br /> Training an AI works similarly to training an animal, although most AIs don't have bodies with which to execute common tasks. Issuing commands to an AI requires an Intelligence + Computers action, and the number of advanced commands one can learn is based on Computers instead of Animal Ken.<br /> <br /> <i>The Beast Mastery Variation (</i><b><i>Shallow Graves</i></b><i>, p. 105) provides a supernatural means of commanding animals, but the system presented here is primarily geared towards players who have purchased the Manticore Companion Merit (</i><b><i>Deviant</i></b><i>, p. 101) or have a pet (potentially one that's a Loyalty Touchstone) and want to teach it to carry out useful tasks. Because this training likely occurs during downtime, that process is largely abstracted, while the act of issuing commands is what carries the risk of failure. In-world, situations in which a command roll is called for are likely to be chaotic and stressful for the animal. From the perspective of a system within a roleplaying game, these are the situations where failure is likely to be more narratively interesting.</i><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-20T19:05:08-08:00","id":1555698,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eric Zawadzki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1555429-black-vans-a-storyteller-s-guide-to-deviant-the-renegades?p=1555679#post1555679\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-size:22px\">Training Animals and Manticores</span><br /> <br /> <b>The Clades Companion</b> (p. 19) divides animals into three broad categories of intelligence: dull, smart, and near-sapient.<br /> <br /> Dull animals can't be trained, as they are creatures of raw instinct incapable of processing human commands. They include reptiles, fish, and invertebrates, as well as many birds.<br /> <br /> Smart animals can learn and so can be trained. Most mammals are smart, as are some birds.<br /> <br /> Near-sapient animals are exceptionally intelligent and capable of solving problems and expressing preferences. Few animals are near-sapient \u2014 dolphins, great apes, and a handful of others.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I understand this is an extreme simplification for game purposes, this is a lot of misconceptions about animal behavior.<br /> <br /> There are definitely reptiles, fish and invertebrates that can be trained IRL to do your Basic Commands. They need specialized training due to species, but still can do it.<br /> <br /> Most animals can be trained in some way to do a variety of tasks, like drug-searching wasps. Maybe not to the extent of a game-defined Command, but they can be trained. Also, most animals can solve problems and express personal preference, they just haven't evolved to communicate those with humans. Honeybees can solve some puzzles and teach other bees, they can even do some math, and they do develop personal preference for some flowers. Ants have a collective intelligence that has been baffling scientists for a time now that we thought of looking into it, astoundingly superior to what would be expected from their individual behavior, and better than humans at some puzzles.<br /> <br /> I'd at the very least give rules for teaching a single specific task to a Dull animal, with no regard for its apparent complexity, especially if the character is an exceptional trainer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-21T05:54:03-08:00","id":1555717,"title":"","content":"It's an oversimplification for inclusion in a TTRPG, for sure. I was building on the Animals chapter of Clades Companion; the categories are more or less verbatim from that. Octopi have been in the news of the weird multiple times for doing absurdly clever things, invertebrates or not. I can see someone making the argument that they're smart or near-sapient, not dull.<br /> <br /> I'm not going to forbid anyone from making the case at the table that <i>their</i> pet spider is capable of a simple, useful trick. Even with ST veto on that, there's always the Manticore Companion Merit (or the Beast Mastery Variation) to override any &quot;a spider can't learn to relay information by skittering around on a piece of paper to select the letters of the words it's trying to say&quot; objections.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-22T04:00:18-08:00","id":1555752,"title":"","content":"The second half of the first chapter focuses on rules hacks for the game's Remade-specific mechanics. This includes an example of a full rework of those systems to remove broken souls from the equation (available for free at the <a href=\"http://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a>), but also guidance on starting characters with additional Experiences.<br /> <br /> <b>Deviant</b> handles character advancement in a weird way as compared to other Chronicles of Darkness games. Because Instability can, in effect, permit a free increase in Variation Magnitude, awarding a few (or many!) Experiences to each character and calling it a day just doesn't cut it. Instead, <b>Black Vans</b> offers the option below. As always, the full text of the second half of the chapter is available to patrons.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Character Creation</span><br /> <br /> Storytellers may adjust the trait allowances for characters during character creation, allowing players to play characters who start out more powerful and experienced than usual.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Extra Experiences</span><br /> <br /> Most traits can be augmented by granting bonus Experiences that may not be used to purchase new Variations, and which may carry additional limits on what traits they can improve. For every 8 extra Experiences, treat the Standing of the cohort as 1 higher when building the conspiracy (or introducing a new one).<br /> <br /> [table]<br /> <br /> Rank Bonus Experiences Effective Standing<br /> <br /> Survivor 8 +1<br /> <br /> Established Renegade 16 +2<br /> <br /> Renegade Role Model 24 +3<br /> <br /> Potential Threat 32 +4<br /> <br /> Serious Threat 40 +5<br /> <br /> Living Legend 48 +6<br /> <br /> Conspiracy Bogeyman 56 +7<br /> <br /> [end table]<br /> <br /> Storytellers should clearly indicate any traits that can't be purchased or improved with bonus Experiences, or are otherwise limited at character creation:<br /> <br /> <b>Acclimation:</b> This is arguably the most powerful trait in the game. Consider restricting initial Acclimation to 2 regardless of starting Experiences (or <b>Shallow Graves</b> Forms) or forbidding the purchase of Acclimation with bonus Experiences entirely. To reflect a cohort that has achieved significant self-mastery, consider awarding one or two dots of Acclimation for free, reducing the bonus Experiences amount accordingly \u2014 capping Acclimation at 3 or 4 or leaving it limited to 2.<br /> <br /> <b>Attributes:</b> These affect Scar Power/Finesse/Resistance, which makes them common purchases in <b>Deviant</b> chronicles. Consider limiting Attribute purchases to no more than half the bonus Experiences. Attributes cannot be increased above 5 dots without the addition of the Superhuman Attribute Variation (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 131).<br /> <br /> <b>Merits:</b> The ten-dot restriction on Merits during character creation normally forces some hard choices regarding these remarkable quirks and capabilities. Consider interactions between Merits (especially ones with an Acclimation prerequisite, such as Learned Magnitude) and any Variations/Scars, as these can become quite potent.<br /> <br /> <b>Skills/Specialties:</b> These reflect a character's mundane capabilities and have minimal impact on Variations or Scars. They make a good way to reflect characters who were elite prior to their Divergence. Skills can't be increased above 5 dots.<br /> <br /> <b>Forms:</b> Some of the advanced Forms in <b>Clades Companion</b> and <b>Shallow Graves</b> can grant inexpensive access to additional Variation Magnitude. Only initial Variation Magnitude and starting Merit dots no greater than Standing can be used to purchase Natural Variations (or additional Variation Magnitude for a Homunculus \u2014 <b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 65)<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Threat Level and Standing</span><br /> <br /> <b>Deviant</b> never explicitly assigns a number value to its named threat levels. A Hyperplasia threat level chronicle features a Standing 2 conspiracy and Renegades with 6 Magnitude of Variations each (including 1 Magnitude from Origin), but depending on how players spend their Experiences, they could average 9 or 11 Magnitude of Variations long before they destroy their initial conspiracy.<br /> <br /> When <b>Black Vans</b> refers to &quot;Threat Level&quot; or &quot;Standing&quot; when talking about the cohort (rather than the character creation process or conspiracy), it is doing so to indicate the average total Magnitude of Variations possessed by the cohort. A Threat Level 1 or Standing 1 cohort is one whose members average fewer than 6 Magnitude of Variations, for example, while a Threat Level 7 or Standing 7 cohort is one whose members average 18-20 Magnitude of Variations (including one from Origin).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">High-Standing</span><br /> <br /> Because starting Variations are measured in Magnitude and not Experiences, they need to be handled separately from other traits. For Renegades with levels of power that exceed even Metamorphosis, Storytellers can use the Standing chart (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 235) to assign Magnitude of Variations and determine the conspiracy's traits. If the conspiracy has Standing 8, for example, each Remade begins with 20 Magnitude of Variations (plus one additional Scar-free Magnitude based on Origin) This works best in a one-shot or short chronicle, as Broken with very large numbers of Variations are often difficult to challenge and have limited options for advancement.<br /> <br /> <b>Magnitude for Experiences:</b> Consider allowing characters to exchange starting Magnitude of Variations for starting Experiences. For every 3 Experiences, reduce starting Variation Magnitude by 1. This never removes the Scar-free Magnitude from Origin.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-25T03:59:30-08:00","id":1555842,"title":"","content":"<b>Shallow Graves</b> explored the character creation process and character arc decisions from a player perspective, the first half of <b>Black Vans</b>' second chapter looks at these traits from a Storyteller's perspective. This includes taking the raw material of character traits and background details and crafting them into an interesting story that will be satisfying to the player running the character. One perennial challenge is a character whose personal history speaks of someone with more skills and knowledge than can be represented by a starting character's Attributes, Skills, and Merits.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Available for free on the Patreon</a> is a discussion of how to convey failure in a way that doesn\u2019t make a character seem inept when their traits support competence or even mastery (as well as the full text of the chapter\u2019s first half, for patrons).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Cooler in the Backstory</span><br /> <br /> Sometimes a character has capabilities or connections in her backstory that are not reflected in her traits. A player wants her to be a brilliant surgeon or a master thief or an Olympic athlete, but the dots just don't add up. One reason this happens is that players often like to play characters who were remarkable even before their Divergence. Mostly, though, this reflects the realities of character creation. Even Remade with the Variations to go toe-to-toe with a Standing 6 conspiracy have the same number of starting Attribute, Skill, and Merit dots as one facing a Standing 1 conspiracy (but see p. XX). At other times, a character's appearance or manner comes with social advantages, stigmas, or both that aren't readily reflected with mundane traits \u2014 such as a very young or very old character. There are several ways to explain this discrepancy:<br /> <br /> <b>That Was a Long Time Ago:</b> The Remade possessed remarkable skills, but they have atrophied with disuse or simple age. At the age of 65, the legendary stunt pilot isn't capable of the aerobatics of her youth, for example.<br /> <br /> <b>Unsung Casualty of Divergence:</b> The Divergence greatly diminished the Broken, stripping him of finely honed skills. This isn't reflected by Scars, simply in the apparently reduced traits implied by his backstory. This adds another layer of tragedy to the Deviant's circumstances.<br /> <br /> <b>That Was Before the Accident:</b> Not everything needs to be blamed on the Divergence. Some injuries leave lasting damage. A torn knee ligament can be career-ending for an athlete, and a severe blow to the head can cause a variety of disorders affecting anything from motor function to emotional regulation.<br /> <br /> <b>Holding Back:</b> The character's capabilities are still there, but circumstances conspire to make them shine less brightly during the early part of the chronicle than they did in their backstory. This works best when the difference between the backstory version of their capabilities and the character creation version isn't huge, or concerns abilities that are unlikely to come into play until later in the chronicle. The player's early Experience expenditures are largely geared towards making the character creation version of the character into the backstory version.<br /> <br /> <b>Natural Variations (Optional Rule):</b> <b>The Clades Companion</b> introduced the concept of Natural Scars and Variations (p. 8), which reflect inherent qualities not tied to the Divergence but which closely resemble Scars and Variations. These are easy to adapt to other purposes, including reflecting a unique mundane capability. Without an appropriate Form, Natural Variations are only available at character creation, must be entangled with an appropriate Scar, can't be of Magnitude 3+, and count against the character's starting Magnitude of Variations. They must be Persistent and Subtle (with the exception of Perpetual Gigantic 1 or Perpetual Miniaturization 1) and must be capabilities a Baseline could conceivably have. They don't count towards limits on Magnitude of Clade Variations (for better or worse) and aren't considered supernatural. Powers that manipulate or shut down supernatural abilities and powers do not affect Natural Variations. This includes Variations and Adaptations that increase or decrease Magnitude or suppress Variations entirely, as well as Instability, so Natural Variations never improve during play. Powers that directly oppose the effects of a Natural Variation do so automatically and without a Clash of Wills.<br /> <br /> <b>Natural Scars (Optional Rule):</b> Natural Scars can be used to reflect a permanent injury, disability, or inherent disadvantage unrelated to the Divergence. They are wholly mundane and not affected by Instability; the only way they may manifest, worsen, or fade after character creation is due to the events of the chronicle (such as an arm being amputated or a child &quot;outgrowing&quot; Omega). Natural Scars must be Persistent, are not counted towards the limits on unentangled Scars, are usually of low Magnitude (rarely 3+), and generate Beats when they create complications for the Remade or her cohort. Without an appropriate Form, Natural Scars cannot entangle Variations.<br /> <br /> [Half Page or Full Page Sidebar]<br /> <br /> <b>Common Natural Scars</b><br /> <br /> Conspicuous Appearance (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 161): Noteworthy tattoos or scars, visible birth defects.<br /> <br /> Dependency (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 162): Prescription drugs to manage a chronic illness.<br /> <br /> Evil Twin<b> (Shallow Graves</b>, p. 122): Earned a terrible reputation or made lasting enemies in the past, or bears an uncanny resemblance to someone who did.<br /> <br /> Genetic Disorder (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 163): Chronic fatigue and various other chronic illnesses.<br /> <br /> Hemophilia (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 164): A bleeding disorder, or use of prescription blood thinners.<br /> <br /> Missing Limb (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 166): Nerve damage, birth defect, amputee, or paralysis.<br /> <br /> Omega (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 125): Child, disability, old age.<br /> <br /> Phobia (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 167): An irrational fear that impacts the character's behavior.<br /> <br /> Sensory Deprivation (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 168): Nearsighted, hard of hearing, blind (in one or both eyes), deaf (in one or both ears).<br /> <br /> Silence (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 168): Speech impediment, deaf, damaged tongue.<br /> <br /> Sleepy (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 127): Chronic fatigue or insomnia, narcolepsy, sleep disorder.<br /> <br /> Sluggish Metabolism (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 168): Old age, chronic illness.<br /> <br /> Thin Skin (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 170): Small child, old age, chronic illness.<br /> <br /> Traumatic Memories (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 128): PTSD.<br /> <br /> <b>Common Natural Variations</b><br /> <br /> Chronological Alignment (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 86): Lucky, gambler, con artist, child, old age.<br /> <br /> Emotion Amplification (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 105): Charlatan, con artist, slimy politician.<br /> <br /> Enhanced Speed (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 117): Martial artist, Olympic runner, legendary traceur.<br /> <br /> Gigantic (Magnitude 1 and Perpetual only; <b>Deviant</b>, p. 119): Giant, NBA/NFL player.<br /> <br /> Hyper-Competence (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 122): An expert in their field.<br /> <br /> Internet Acuity (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 96): Master hacker.<br /> <br /> Miniaturization (Magnitude 1 and Perpetual only; <b>Deviant</b>, p. 126): Child, little person.<br /> <br /> Shadows of the Past (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 129): Master detective or forensics specialist.<br /> <br /> Superhuman Attribute (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 131): Paragon of inherent talent.<br /> <br /> Tool Mastery (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 91): Contract killer, master craftsperson.<br /> <br /> Vocalization (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 103): Ventriloquist, opera singer.<br /> <br /> [End Sidebar]","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-27T04:00:44-08:00","id":1555907,"title":"","content":"If the first half of Chapter Two focuses on building a chronicle around the players' character sheets, the second half explores ways to motivate a cohort once play has begun. This includes today's preview, which provides potential answers to an important question: &quot;How do I keep the Renegades from killing off my villains too quickly?&quot;<br /> <br /> The bonus free preview on <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon </a>explores ways to motivate a cohort without leaning on conspiracies and Touchstones to do so (and the full text of the rest of the chapter is available to patrons).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Conviction Survival</span><br /> <br /> Once a Conviction Touchstone enters the chronicle \u2014 whether he was on a character sheet from the start or is someone who earned the Renegades' ire along the way \u2014 quite often one of the Storyteller's greatest challenges is keeping the cohort from killing him before he can serve as a proper antagonist. Endowed as they are with superhuman abilities, a group of Deviants can overcome most individual opponents who attempt to stand against them. A Storyteller can deploy many different techniques to ensure that a Conviction Touchstone's first on-screen appearance isn't his last:<br /> <br /> <b>Obscurity:</b> The Touchstone is the hidden mastermind behind the plots against the cohort, such that the connection between her and the events she orchestrates may approach undetectable. She could very well arrange red herrings so that others are blamed for the events she orchestrated. Merely confirming her involvement poses a challenge, and as long as she's far enough off-screen, bringing her to the rude justice of violent demise is off the table.<br /> <br /> <b>Absence:</b> The villain remains off-screen but makes no secret of his involvement. He may simply send the cohort threatening messages, whether they're packages delivered by a courier who has no connection to the conspiracy or phone calls delivered to bystanders who hand one of the Renegades a phone and nervously explain that &quot;it's for you&quot;. If some conspiracy presence is needed, he works through intermediaries who do his dirty work for him \u2014 and who take any lumps the Remade may dish out in retaliation. Often, these are conspiracy goons, but they're nearly as likely to be unwitting catspaws or even innocent bystanders manipulated into the position by the Touchstone's machinations. Killing these proxies does no lasting harm to the cohort's enemy or the conspiracy he serves.<br /> <br /> <b>Leverage:</b> If the Conviction Touchstone must be present, it's safest for her to do so when it's clear to the Renegades that the cost of opening hostilities against her is much greater than any advantage gained from doing so. It might involve a hostage, the remedy to a problem the cohort can't solve without the villain's help, information they can't get anywhere else, or dire warnings that if they hurt her it will put them in the sights of law enforcement or provoke a strong reaction from the conspiracy she serves. The first of these works best if the antagonist can both prove that the hostage is within her power (whether through physical proximity or a live feed that can serve as proof of life) and that her inaction (rather than her action) will cause the victim to be harmed. This is what's known as a dead man's switch, and is used to disincentivize ambushes and unexpected heroics. For leverage to work, the cohort must believe she has it, must believe her capable of making good on her threats, and must regard the threatened consequences as worse than allowing the Conviction Touchstone to have her way.<br /> <br /> <b>Alliance:</b> Rather than resorting to threats, which tend to put even Baselines on edge, to say nothing of Broken, the Conviction Touchstone reaches out his hand in friendship \u2014 whether temporary or lasting, feigned or genuine. This works best if he can offer the cohort something they want (or need) even more than his blood, such as help taking down a worse villain, committing an act of sabotage against the heart of the conspiracy, or working together to escape enemies who have imprisoned them both. As with leverage, this works best if killing the Conviction Touchstone will create significant complications for the cohort that his continued survival allows them to avoid. The danger of this strategy is that he's trying to broker a truce or alliance with <i>Broken</i>. Even if the cohort agrees that he's more useful to them as a friend than as an enemy, it only takes one dissenter to leave the Conviction Touchstone bleeding out in the snow of the frozen wasteland they've all been stranded in.<br /> <br /> <b>Repentance:</b> The Conviction Touchstone sees (or at least <i>pretends</i> to see) the error of their ways. They make apologies, promise to abandon bad behavior patterns, and otherwise say whatever they must to convince the Deviants that they aren't such a bad person after all \u2014 or at least not one that is beyond redemption. This usually reads as insincere and desperate if the cohort has their enemy in their power; a villain pleading for their life when staring death in the face seldom convinces a neutral witness, much less overcomes the all-consuming loathing of the Remade for a Conviction Touchstone. It's more effective if this repentance comes after a show of good faith, such as if they rescue the Broken or her Loyalty Touchstone or commit some act of radical defiance against the conspiracy. Clever Conviction Touchstones might orchestrate assassination attempts they can thwart dramatically in hopes of forcing a reversal from Conviction to Loyalty \u2014 or to at least lower the priority the Deviant places on their death.<br /> <br /> <b>Retreat:</b> Linchpins who survive their first few encounters with Renegades quickly learn that the best place to be in a fight with Remade is literally anywhere else. An antagonist can put in a brief, on-screen appearance to issue ultimatums, make threats, or bark orders at conspiracy agents and still leave as soon as actual violence erupts \u2014 or preferably <i>before</i>. She must assume that her enemies will prioritize her, and so any escape must make it difficult to follow her. It could be a secret passage, an idling car, or a waiting helicopter, and is almost always accompanied by a large number of goons or an impending catastrophe that draws the cohort's attention away from her long enough for her to make her escape.<br /> <br /> <b>Ruthlessness:</b> When violence is likely or inevitable, a smart villain takes pains to stack the deck in his favor. This could take the form of numerous and heavily armed backup, fortifications and controlled environments that afford a home-field advantage, or advanced personal equipment and Icons. If the Conviction Touchstone is a Deviant or has other occult connections, his advantages likely include supernatural powers. Likewise, exploiting the cohort's Scars or using their known proclivities can strengthen his negotiating power. Enough of these taken together and prominently displayed can convince the Renegades not to resort to violence in the first place.<br /> <br /> <b>Possum:</b> A Renegade is usually single-minded where his Conviction Touchstones are concerned. He'll often fight against overwhelming odds if there is a chance he can land the killing blow on one. An antagonist can use this to her advantage by letting the Deviant have what he wants \u2014 or at least allowing him to think he has gotten it. Whether a hidden bulletproof vest wards away the deadly shot or she reacts to a glancing blow as though suffering a mortal wound, going down and then <i>staying</i> down can preserve her from the dreaded double-tap. This works best if the cohort doesn't have the luxury of sticking around to check for a pulse, such as if there are a lot of armed goons still standing or if the environment is unstable or dangerous.<br /> <br /> <b>Surrender:</b> When violence fails, and escape proves impossible, unexpected surrender can forestall death at Renegade hands. Deviants, although not renowned for their mercy, need their Conviction Touchstones, and destroying one is most satisfying on the Remade's own terms. Many Broken entertain fantasies of revenge for years, and putting a hasty bullet in the back of their Progenitor's head before the cops show up may seem insufficiently poetic. This tactic is a dangerous one. It can lead to a lengthy and painful imprisonment. However, a Linchpin with any amount of foresight has contingency plans to arrange for his rescue or extraction from enemy hands, assuming he has no discreet means of making good his escape when his captors' backs are turned.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-29T04:15:57-08:00","id":1555951,"title":"","content":"Chapter Three concerns the occupants of all those black vans, whether they're Baseline agents or Devoted. The first half presents some simple templates and a bunch of randomized tables to serve as creative prompts for antagonists, allies, and foils \u2013 whether because the Storyteller is whipping up a character on the fly or just feeling uninspired when preparing for the next gaming session. Today I'm sharing those simplified templates.<br /> <br /> The free bonus excerpt on the <a href=\"http://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a> is a summary of how Variation randomization works (and full text with all the tables a Storyteller could want is available to patrons).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Traits</span><br /> <br /> Here we provide a method by which a Storyteller may quickly create a Baseline or Deviant character \u2014 or simply select Variations and/or Scars.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Basics</span><br /> <br /> Categorize the character you wish to introduce as Important, Significant, Insignificant, or Bystander. This will determine the numerical value of traits.<br /> <br /> <b>Attributes:</b> Assign Primary/Secondary/Tertiary Attributes to either Mental/Physical/Social or Power/Resistance/Finesse categories. Given a 3/2/2 array, for example, you could choose to place threes in Physical Attributes (Strength, Dexterity, and Stamina) and twos in Mental and Social Attributes, or threes in Finesse Attributes (Wits, Dexterity, and Manipulation) and twos in Power and Resistance Attributes.<br /> <br /> <b>Dice Pools:</b> Storyteller characters usually don't track Skills. Instead, they have a few categories of things they're good at doing (such as Guns, Friends Everywhere, or Obscure Lore). For these, the character doesn't suffer untrained penalties and receives a bonus based on whether they're an Important, Significant, Insignificant, or Bystander character. For all other actions, use their relevant Attribute and apply an untrained penalty.<br /> <br /> <b>Initiative Modifier:</b> This is equal to Finesse + Resistance or Physical + Social, minus any penalties for weapons.<br /> <br /> <b>Defense:</b> Usually equal to Finesse or the lower of Mental or Physical. If the character has combat dice pools, use the <i>higher</i> of Wits or Dexterity and add 1.<br /> <br /> <b>Health:</b> This is equal to Size (usually 5) + Resistance/Physical.<br /> <br /> <b>Willpower:</b> This is equal to either Mental + Social or 2 \u00d7 Resistance. However, most Storyteller characters have limitations on how much they can spend during a scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Speed:</b> This is equal to either Power + Finesse + 5 or (2 \u00d7 Physical) + 5.<br /> <br /> <b>Other Traits:</b> If the character is a Deviant, they will also have Acclimation, Magnitude of Variations, Scars, and Adaptations. Significant and Important characters (and some Insignificant ones) will have Merits.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Bystander</span><br /> <br /> Bystander characters are present in most scenes but don't usually want to be involved. Narratively, they exist to get in the way, serve as hostages, or cause complications more by dint of being there than by any action they might take. A Bystander character might pick up a fallen gun and take a desperate shot, but their chances of scoring a hit are small \u2014 although they can add chaos to a scene. Deviants are never Bystander characters, and even faceless conspiracy goons are more capable within their areas of competence.<br /> <br /> <b>Traits</b><br /> <br /> <b>Attributes:</b> 2/2/2<br /> <br /> <b>Dice Pools:</b> Most of the time, Bystander characters don't have skills relevant to the scene they're in. If it seems reasonable that they would have some training or equipment, give them four dice with those actions (for example, a mall cop attempting to restrain and subdue).<br /> <br /> <b>Initiative Modifier:</b> +4<br /> <br /> <b>Defense:</b> 2<br /> <br /> <b>Health:</b> 7<br /> <br /> <b>Willpower:</b> Bystander characters can't spend Willpower.<br /> <br /> <b>Speed:</b> 9<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Insignificant</span><br /> <br /> Insignificant characters usually only appear in a single scene. Most Goons are Insignificant. Their Attribute arrays, dice pools and Variations (if Deviants) are usually based either on their Scar (if they're Remade) or on the type of scene they feature in.<br /> <br /> <b>Traits</b><br /> <br /> <b>Attributes:</b> 3/2/2<br /> <br /> <b>Dice Pools:</b> Attribute + 2 in three areas of expertise.<br /> <br /> <b>Willpower:</b> Insignificant characters can only spend one point of Willpower per scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Acclimation:</b> If the character is a Deviant, they have Acclimation 0. However, the Storyteller may reduce an Attribute category by one to increase Acclimation by one.<br /> <br /> <b>Magnitude of Variations:</b> Standing + 2.<br /> <br /> <b>Adaptations:</b> Insignificant Deviants have but won't use Adaptations.<br /> <br /> <b>Merits:</b> Up to three dots of Merits.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Mini-Bosses</span><br /> <br /> Whether they're elite Goons or disposable (but potent) Devoted, some Insignificant characters are a cut above the rank-and-file. To reflect this, increase their effective Standing by no more than the number of members of the cohort. You may exchange some of this additional Standing for Experiences at a rate of 8 Experiences per 1 Standing, spending these on Adaptations (3 Experiences each), a bonus to an Attribute (4 Experiences each), and Acclimation/Forms/Merits (at their normal cost).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Significant</span><br /> <br /> Significant characters are ones the Storyteller expects to become recurring characters \u2014 although this doesn't always mean they'll survive first contact with the cohort. They might be an occasional ally, a Progenitor's lieutenant, a troublesome monster hunter, or anyone else who might make a guest appearance in a chapter or two each story or could drive a story themself.<br /> <br /> Don't feel compelled to assign every trait from the start. Particularly when introducing a character extemporaneously, even if you hope they'll become a regular feature of the chronicle, it usually suffices to assign only what you need to have figured out for the current scene. You can flesh out additional areas of expertise, Variations, and Merits later.<br /> <br /> <b>Traits</b><br /> <br /> You may exchange Standing for Experiences at a rate of 8 Experiences per 1 Standing, spending these freely (but not on Variations).<br /> <br /> <b>Attributes:</b> 4/3/2<br /> <br /> <b>Dice Pools:</b> Attribute + 3 in five areas of expertise.<br /> <br /> <b>Willpower:</b> Significant characters can spend up to three points of Willpower per scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Acclimation:</b> If the character is a Deviant, they have Acclimation 1.<br /> <br /> <b>Variations:</b> If the character is a Deviant, they have Magnitude of Variations based on Standing (3 Magnitude of Variations at Standing 1, 6 at Standing 2, 8 at Standing 3, etc.).<br /> <br /> <b>Adaptations:</b> Significant characters have Adaptations as normal. It's simplest to use the defaults in <b>Deviant</b>.<br /> <br /> <b>Merits:</b> Up to five dots of Merits.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Important</span><br /> <br /> Important characters are those the Storyteller expects to be a central feature of the chronicle, such as a mentor, rival, or major antagonist. It's appropriate to build these with full character sheets. This doesn't preclude using random generation tables as a creative prompt but using them exclusively from start to finish will probably result in an unfocused concept \u2014 especially for high Standing Broken.<br /> <br /> As with Significant characters, it's okay not to have all the details worked out from the start.<br /> <br /> <b>Traits</b><br /> <br /> If creating an Important character during a chapter, use the following process and then fill out a complete character sheet later. This will mean the final character will usually have significantly lower Attributes than he does when you first introduce him.<br /> <br /> You may exchange Standing for Experiences at a rate of 8 Experiences per 1 Standing, spending these freely (but not on Variations).<br /> <br /> <b>Attributes:</b> 5/3/2<br /> <br /> <b>Dice Pools:</b> Attribute + 3 in up to seven areas of expertise.<br /> <br /> <b>Willpower:</b> Normal Willpower, spent as desired.<br /> <br /> <b>Acclimation:</b> If the character is a Deviant, they have Acclimation 1.<br /> <br /> <b>Variations:</b> Variation Magnitude based on Standing (3 Magnitude of Variations at Standing 1, 6 at Standing 2, 8 at Standing 3, etc.).<br /> <br /> <b>Adaptations:</b> Important characters have Adaptations, whether the defaults or alternative ones from <b>Shallow Graves</b>.<br /> <br /> <b>Merits:</b> Up to 10 dots of Merits.<br /> <br /> <b>Experiences:</b> For major antagonists or other powerful figures, apply an Acquainted/Personal/Obsessive template (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 236) or increase effective Standing by no more than the number of members of the cohort. Effective Standing can exceed 10 but any effective Standing in excess of 10 must be exchanged for Experiences.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-02T04:13:26-08:00","id":1556091,"title":"","content":"Whereas the first half of Chapter Three concentrated on randomizing the traits of Storyteller characters, the second half does so for their narrative qualities. Today I'm sharing the first several creative prompts for describing Goons, although they could be used to build other Storyteller characters you might need to drop into your chronicle.<br /> <br /> The free preview on the <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon </a>does the same for some of the 100 conspiracy agents you will meet if you fight the Web of Pain long enough (with full text of the second half of the chapter available to patrons).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Goons</span><br /> <br /> Although the word &quot;goon&quot; is frequently paired with the word &quot;faceless&quot;, making the rank-and-file antagonists of a <b>Deviant</b> chronicle indistinguishable from one another is boring and kills a potential element of horror. Goons are people, and while their quirky tastes in witty t-shirts and fondness for the music of Beethoven in no way absolve them of culpability for the atrocities they commit and enable, these details can draw attention to the collateral damage the conspiracy inflicts by cavalierly placing these people in mortal danger to boost a bottom line or further some higher-up's personal agenda.<br /> <br /> Despite their relevance to Goons specifically, any of the descriptions below could be applied to Baseline agents, Devoted, or even Storyteller characters with no connection to the Web of Pain. When used to design a conspiracy's Goons, consider ways to establish conformity and eccentricity with your descriptions.<br /> <br /> <b>Conformity:</b> The minions of a conspiracy often have a certain sameness to them. It might be a uniform or logo they all wear or emblazon on their equipment. It could be a requirement that they all wear their hair short and go cleanshaven. Possibly, they favor a particular model of van or gun. Consider the ways in which the conspiracy's agents resemble one another. Try to pick at least some traits that hint at the nature of the conspiracy or how they treat their help. Military-style uniforms and well-maintained equipment speak to the organization's orderliness and internal discipline. Casual attire and unkempt appearances suggest the opposite, and something strange like a strong cologne or matching drug paraphernalia is likely to raise eyebrows and invite questions. A particular presentation could apply to all agents of a conspiracy, those that belong to a specific division or Node within it, or only to those in the current scene. A single squad of a private army could be visibly exhausted because they just finished a grueling forced march, for example, or a specialized kill team might all wear skull-like masks and tactical gloves to intimidate those who might be tempted to defy them.<br /> <br /> <b>Eccentricity:</b> In a conspiracy of indistinguishable assholes, no two goons are truly identical. Some are taller or shorter, have scars or tattoos, carry equipment the others don't have, or are otherwise distinct from one another. Consider giving each goon (or other character in the scene) a distinguishing trait.<br /> <br /> [H3]Example Traits[/H3]<br /> <b>01-02 Armed:</b> He is carrying or brandishing one or more weapons. It might be a firearm, whether pistol, rifle, shotgun, assault rifle, or automatic weapon. It could instead be a hand-to-hand weapon, such as a knife or cudgel. It could be crude (a shiv, length of chain, or pipe), archaic (a sword or bow), or tricked out with a laser sight or high-tech gizmos meant to improve its range and accuracy. He might also sport a belt or bandolier of spare ammunition or grenades (whether frag, flashbang, or white phosphorous).<br /> <br /> <b>03-04 Athletic Equipment:</b> She wears or brandishes athletic equipment. She might be kitted out for football, dressed for a soccer game, or look like she's about to step onto a tennis court or into a gymnastics meet. She might carry a golf club, baseball bat, polo mallet, or rodeo lasso \u2014 whether as a tool for the sport or with lethal intent (and in the latter case, it might be modified for increased lethality, such as being wrapped in barbed wire or studded with nails).<br /> <br /> <b>05-06 Bag:</b> She carries a bag or sack. It might be a purse \u2014 whether an elegant little handbag, a practical container for keys, wallet, phone, and assorted essentials, or one so large it could have practically anything inside. Or maybe it's a backpack, whether a school bag, camping rucksack, or rugged military backpack. Possibly, it's a paper, plastic, or reusable grocery bag \u2014 or just a sack lunch (or paper bag of dollar bills). It could be a messenger bag, duffel, or fanny pack. It could contain only practical necessities but might include keepsakes, family photographs, a book, or other hints about her inner life that the cohort can discover.<br /> <br /> <b>07-08 Belt:</b> He wears a belt, suspenders, harness, or other contraption of straps and buckles. It might serve the simple, practical purpose of holding his pants up. It could be a means of keeping the tools of his trade close to hand, whether it's a gun, sword, knives, or other weapons, or the tools of a carpenter, plumber, or electrician. It could instead be decorative or a sign of rank \u2014 whether it's a prize-winner's rodeo belt or a martial arts belt. A harness might likewise be purely aesthetic, but it's more likely a safety apparatus to protect him when climbing, scuba diving, or parachuting.<br /> <br /> <b>09-10 Beverage:</b> She has a drink in her hand or on a nearby table. Her particular poison could be alcohol (whether in an elegant glass, beer bottle, or paper bag), tea, coffee (in a mug or a clear plastic or cardboard cup), juice, soda, milk, bottled water, or more unusual beverages. She might offer drinks when hosting guests or trying to win someone over. She may also have noteworthy disposal habits; perhaps she tosses empty cans on the ground, meticulously crushes them with her hands, or diligently rinses them out before putting them in the recycling bin.<br /> <br /> <b>11-12 Body Shape:</b> He is notably taller, shorter, thinner, fatter, or more muscular than average. Or perhaps he has a distinctively shaped face, nose, mouth, chin, or ears.<br /> <br /> <b>13-14 Consuming Media:</b> She is looking at or listening to something. It might be a radio (or its contemporary equivalent), whether playing music, talk, audiobook, or podcast. She could be watching a movie, TV show (whether a game show, talk show, soap opera, sitcom, or drama), or a web video (whether short, long form, series, or stream). Perhaps she's looking at social media \u2014 whether it's text, images, gifs, or videos. Possibly she's reading a book (print or electronic, fiction or nonfiction, text or graphic) or news (print or online; mainstream, niche, or extremist). Maybe she's playing a game (card, board, or video; casual or hardcore; solo, versus, or co-op).<br /> <br /> <b>15-16 Contraption:</b> They're carrying, wearing, or using a strange device. It could be a high-tech weapon, powered exosuit, or scanner. Perhaps it's biological, with flesh, blood, and pumping lungs, and is somehow both alive and inanimate. Maybe it's magical or otherwise arcane, such as a mystic staff, glowing amulet, or book of spells. Maybe they wield it with confidence, perhaps they have some idea what they're doing but can't fully control it, or possibly they have no clue how it works and are just pushing buttons and praying one of them does something useful.<br /> <br /> <b>17-18 Conveyance:</b> They have a means of transporting themself either near at hand or on their person. It could be skates (or a skateboard), a bicycle, or a scooter (kick, electric, or motorized). It might be a consumer motorcycle, car, or truck. It may be a construction, farm, or military vehicle. Possibly, it's a horse, donkey, or other riding animal. It could even be a wingsuit or jetpack.<br /> <br /> <b>19-20 Costume:</b> She wears a costume. It could be a disguise to conceal her identity or to create the illusion that she is someone she is not. Or perhaps it's suitable for a costume party, sci-fi convention, or movie shoot. It could imply a general type or person or creature, such as a gangster or cat girl, or it could refer to a specific celebrity or character from a TV show or movie.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-04T04:19:19-08:00","id":1556141,"title":"","content":"People have tinkered with crossover between Chronicles of Darkness games for as long as they've had two splats' core books to rub together. The temptation is understandable. Although the themes and power levels vary considerably from one game to another, they all share the same core Storytelling system. Also, some players are plain more excited about playing as one kind of monster than as another, and sometimes a Storyteller allows this in the interest of ensuring that player's participation (or just because the concept is so awesome that she can't resist).<br /> <br /> For a long time, Chronicles of Darkness games didn't deliberately encourage this. Some made mention of other splats but usually portrayed them as potential Storyteller characters or external factions rather than as potential characters players would run. Beast was the first attempt at creating a game whose protagonists could serve as a bridge between splats. The later Dark Eras books featured multiple splats in the same era, which although arguably intended as an &quot;or&quot;, often served as an &quot;and&quot;. The Contagion Chronicle was the only supplement to fully embrace crossover, and while the release of a book about supernatural diseases couldn't have had more unfortunate timing, I thought the basic premise was really cool.<br /> <br /> When Deviant came out, I heard a lot of comments about how it could be used to create &quot;pseudo-splats&quot; \u2013 monsters with origins in the same myths as the other core books but with a different direction. It enabled werewolves whose transformation was a monthly curse, for example, or vampires with much-simplified mechanics (making them suitable for Storyteller characters or troupes that didn't own a copy of Requiem). Of course, then people came to the Discords with questions about how you'd do a mage in Deviant, and I could only laugh uncomfortably (because the answer is &quot;that's really hard&quot;).<br /> <br /> I also heard a lot of observations about how members of a lot of other splats could run Web of Pain conspiracies. Ordo Dracul, alchemists, Seers of the Throne, and more could easily wind up at the head of a conspiracy \u2013 or at least participating as agents within one. Although the official position was that there wasn't enough space in the core to address this overlap (the Pleseus Guild was specifically name-checked in the first draft of the core book's fiction, in fact, but we cut it to avoid immediately implying crossover that we wouldn't be addressing), I couldn't deny that they had a good point. You'd have to figure out a way to account for variations in power level between different game lines, but as a narrative went, it was a reasonable one.<br /> <br /> When I sat down to consider what a crossover section would look like in a Deviant Storyteller's guide, I ultimately settled on a few different goals:<br /> <br /> 1. For troupes with access to one or more of the other Chronicles of Darkness games, I wanted to provide architecture for crossover play, whether the chronicle focused on the Remade (or on another game line's protagonists), had a fairly equal mix of two or three creature types, or was a complete monster mash.<br /> <br /> 2. For troupes who lacked those books, I wanted to provide enough information to introduce a &quot;lite&quot; version of a vampire, werewolf, or other creature \u2013 whether it was one played by the Storyteller (recurring ally/antagonist/foil or monster of the week) or one of the players.<br /> <br /> 3. I wanted to address ways to include other splats in a Deviant chronicle, including the roles they might play in the Web of Pain.<br /> <br /> 4. I wanted to use the exercise to introduce new optional systems that Storytellers could borrow, modify, or use as starting points for rules specific to their own chronicles.<br /> <br /> That was a <i>lot</i> to pack into a Storyteller's guide, especially given I'd need to do it for each of 10 different game lines. I couldn't deliver the full Mage or Demon or Changeling experience in the ~3,500 words I gave myself for each splat, but it's now a lot easier to build a vampire or a cursed werewolf or a local sorcerer who sometimes helps the cohort than it was when I started.<br /> <br /> Today's other free Patreon sample shows off an example of one of the Lesser Forms associated with Beasts (and patrons get the full text of the chapter's introduction, as well as the Beast and Changeling write-ups).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Web of Pain</span><br /> <br /> Many monsters successfully integrate into the Web of Pain, and not all of them are Baselines or Remade. Conversely, some monsters have their own counterparts to the Web of Pain, which might coexist with it, oppose it, or even integrate with it. Supernatural creatures who find themselves in positions of power in a conspiracy are much less likely to be its <i>de facto</i> prisoners than are Devoted. However, they fill a similar role mechanically:<br /> <br /> \u2022 Supernatural creatures can be added to a conspiracy's roster of occult agents and assigned to a Node with an alternative to the Recruit Devoted Project (<b>Devoted Companion</b>, p. 29) that is specific to their particular splat (such as when using Privileges).<br /> <br /> \u2022 A conspiracy that has occult agents can draw upon their connections to their own kind to include functionally identical versions of them in a Send Agents action, using the Shock Troops effect (<b>Devoted Companion</b>, p. 23), except specific to that splat. Those who survive the scene return to their usual business after lending their assistance.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Threat Level</span><br /> <br /> Like Remade, occult beings are built with a Threat Level in mind. Each supernatural type has a default Threat Level based on its capabilities and vulnerabilities at character creation. Compare the Threat Level of the chronicle with the Threat Level of the creature:<br /> <br /> \u2022 If the creature's Threat Level is equal to that of the chronicle, the character is a typical &quot;starting character&quot; for that type of occult being. If the troupe has access to the game line that features that creature, players (or Storytellers) who wish to play one may use the character creation rules for that game. If the troupe lacks the game or wishes to use simplified, Deviant-focused systems, start with a Baseline character (i.e., skip Step Six of the character creation process in the <b>Deviant </b>core book) and apply the appropriate Greater Form and all its Lesser Forms.<br /> <br /> \u2022 If the creature's Threat Level is less than that of the chronicle, apply the template/Forms as above first. For each point by which the chronicle's Threat Level exceeds the creature's, the creature may spend up to 8 Experiences to expand its capabilities. A creature type that is Threat Level 3 in a Threat Level 5 chronicle, for example, has all its Greater and Lesser Forms and can spend 16 Experiences.<br /> <br /> \u2022 If the creature's Threat Level is greater than that of the chronicle, do not apply the full template/Form (i.e., skip Step Six of the character creation process). Instead, apply the Greater Form and a number of the creature type's Lesser Forms equal to the chronicle's Threat Level. The character may purchase the other Lesser Forms for 8 Experiences each. If the troupe has and is using the game line that features the creature type, he enjoys the full benefits of the splat once he has purchased all its Lesser Forms.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Greater and Lesser Forms</span><br /> <br /> Occult creatures have at least one Greater Form and one Lesser Form. Some also have Scar Forms.<br /> <br /> A <b>Greater Form</b> represents a true non-Deviant character. Except under highly unusual narrative circumstances, a character should only ever have one Greater Form, which includes the Deviant template itself.<br /> <br /> A <b>Lesser Form</b> represents a fraction of the power of a Greater Form \u2015 approximately equal to one Threat Level. A character can have any number of Lesser Forms, but all Lesser Forms should come from the same supernatural creature type (except with Storyteller permission and a strong narrative explanation). A Threat Level 3 creature, for example, usually has three Lesser Forms. If purchased after character creation, a Lesser Form costs 8 Experiences.<br /> <br /> While all supernatural creatures have some inherent weaknesses, some have a <b>Scar Form</b>. This is a package of serious vulnerabilities that partially offsets the advantages the being's occult nature grants. It acts as a reversed Lesser Form, offsetting the Experiences or Threat Level cost of one Lesser Form. A Scar Form can't increase a character's starting Variation Magnitude, only reduce the cost of Lesser Forms within the same creature type.<br /> <br /> For example, Prometheans are Threat Level 3 because they have four Lesser Forms and one Scar Form. A character with the Promethean Scar Form and two Lesser Forms would be Threat Level 1, while one with all four Lesser Forms and no Scar Form would be Threat Level 4.<br /> <br /> <b>Non-Deviants and Beats:</b> Although occult creatures have some inherent disadvantages, none emphasize Scars nearly to the degree that Broken do. Each game line has its own criteria for when characters earn Beats, but we don't have space to reproduce them all here. For that reason, if a character has a Greater Form, consider using these general rules of thumb for when to award a Beat:<br /> <br /> \u2022 All characters earn a Beat at the end of a chapter.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Early in a chronicle (or soon after introducing a character with a Greater Form), award a Beat whenever the character behaves in a way that draws attention to the ways in which they are different from Deviants \u2015 drinking the blood of an unconscious enemy (for a vampire), for example, or asking probing questions about what it means to be human (for a Promethean).<br /> <br /> \u2022 Award a Beat whenever the character acts on a trait in a way that creates a complication for themself or their cohort. This could mean adhering to an Anchor, interacting with a Touchstone, or using a power in a way that endangers the character or bystanders.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-06T04:03:43-08:00","id":1556232,"title":"","content":"Until Deviant came along, Demon was the game line for which I had written the most words, so it will always have a special place in my heart. Compressing the mechanics of demonic forms was an interesting challenge, but I think the parts I had the most fun with were the bits that interact with Cover, which is what I'm sharing today.<br /> <br /> The bonus free Patreon excerpt has some Geist goodness for you (with full text for both splats available for patrons).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Integrity Trait: Cover</span><br /> <br /> Every demon is an impostor pretending to be human. The Unchained has a human body, human relationships, a job, possessions, and all the other trappings of humanity, but it's all an illusion. A demon's Cover rating reflects how successfully his stolen or cobbled-together life holds up to scrutiny \u2014 whether he has a birth certificate, a social media presence, old teachers who remember what kind of student he was, and so forth.<br /> <br /> A demon's Cover rating starts at 5 and can be as high as 10. If the demon's Cover rating falls to 0, he loses the active Cover permanently (the Sanctity of Merits applies) but resets Cover to 5; if he loses his last Cover (or if he lacks both the Demon Greater Form and the Legend Lesser Form and loses his only Cover), he can no longer hide his demonic nature and gains the Catastrophic Glitch Condition (p. XX).<br /> <br /> As long as the Unchained plays the role reflected by the Cover he currently wears, he finds it easy to hide his nature. He compromises his Cover when he acts out of character, whether it's telling Baselines that he's a demon, exhibiting skills inconsistent with his artificial backstory, or turning into a biomechanical monstrosity in full view of witnesses or cameras. Whenever he compromises his Cover, the demon rolls Wits + Manipulation.<br /> <br /> <i>Likely Modifiers: High Cover rating (+1 if 6-7, +2 if 8-10) or low Cover rating (\u20131 if 2-3, \u20132 if 1). Exhibited overt supernatural powers (\u20132) or took demonic form (\u20133) while in the presence of Baselines. Potential witnesses were drunk, high, or in another state that might lead them to disbelieve their senses (+1).</i><br /> <br /> <b>Roll Results</b><br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Success:</b> The demon gains the Minor Glitch Condition (p. XX).<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Exceptional Success:</b> The demon increases her Cover rating by 1 (to a maximum of 10) or regains a point of Willpower. Either way, she takes a Beat.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Failure:</b> The demon loses a dot of Cover and gains the Minor Glitch Condition.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Dramatic Failure:</b> The demon loses a dot of Cover and gains the Major Glitch Condition (p. XX). She also takes a Beat.<br /> <br /> <b>Compromise:</b> The use of many demonic powers constitutes an automatic compromise, triggering a compromise roll as usual (or one with an additional \u20133 penalty if the power is a &quot;major compromise&quot;). Unchained with the Demon Greater Form or the Legend Lesser Form roll Wits + Manipulation normally. A character who lacks either Form instead suffers the Minor Glitch Condition (p. XX) \u2014 or the Major Glitch Condition (p. XX), if it is a major compromise.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Lesser Form: Legend</span><br /> <br /> This Form reflects the demon's ability to adopt a perfect human disguise and integrate herself into society \u2014 or a Deviant's supernaturally reinforced alter ego. It has several effects:<br /> <br /> <b>Cover:</b> The Unchained has a complete Baseline persona with all the details of a life lived from birth to the present. The demon is always considered to be &quot;present&quot; in the life of his Cover; even if he takes a weeklong trip to investigate a God-Machine project, his coworkers won't notice his absence, his friends will automatically insert him into their memories of any social gatherings he would have attended, and his children will remember him telling them bedtime stories. He has the Cover Integrity trait, and this is in addition to his normal Integrity trait if he lacks the Demon Greater Form.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Legend:</b> Once per chapter, when the Unchained needs access to a Skill or Merit in which he has no dots but which is consistent with his current Cover's identity, he may gain three dots in any combination of appropriate Skills and Merits until the end of the scene.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Multiple Covers:</b> A demon with multiple Covers may switch between them once per chapter as an instant action taken while he is alone and unobserved. The new Cover replaces his current appearance in his present location; he doesn't teleport himself to where the artificial person would be.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Embodiment:</b> Once per scene, if the demon's behavior remains consistent with his current Cover and it creates a significant complication for himself or his allies, he increases his Cover rating by 1 (to a maximum of 10). This cannot create a new Cover.<br /> <br /> <b>Emotional Control:</b> The Unchained has perfect control over her physiological responses to her emotions. All attempts to judge the demon's emotional state, detect lies, or assess desires based on her involuntary physical indicators automatically fail, instead reading what the Unchained chooses to express. This even affects supernatural attempts to discern the demon's emotions, desires, or candor.<br /> <br /> <b>Natural Aptitude:</b> The demon receives the Eidetic Memory Merit (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 96) for free and is fluent in all human languages that have native speakers.<br /> <br /> <b>Spoof:</b> Whenever the Unchained is subjected to an effect that would potentially reveal her supernatural nature, she may choose to &quot;read&quot; as a Baseline.<br /> <br /> This Form grants a single Cover. Each additional Cover costs 4 Experiences.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Lesser Form: Unchained Society</span><br /> <br /> Escaping the God-Machine's service is a beginning. Avoiding its attention is progress. Building a society of Unchained is success, and the demon has established herself as a part of that society.<br /> <br /> <b>Aetheric Resonance:</b> The demon recognizes Infrastructure and Icons when she perceives them with her own senses, and she can automatically sense an expenditure of Aether, the use of angelic powers, or the activation of a God-Machine project within 100 yards/meters. Once per chapter, the Unchained may either determine the general intent of Infrastructure/Icons (or relevant demon/angel/God-Machine powers) or extend her perception of active use of powers to one kilometer until the end of the scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Agenda:</b> Unchained motives for defecting and refusing to return to the God-Machine fall into four broad categories. The demon chooses one:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Inquisitors</b> seek to understand some part of the God-Machine's operation, whether to keep clear of its servants or to use that knowledge as a bargaining chip with other Unchained.<br /> <br /> <i>Once per chapter, the Inquisitor may suggest a connection between two seemingly unrelated things and have them be related (subject to Storyteller approval), or she may add +3 to a Mental Skill roll.</i><br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Integrators</b> wish to serve the God-Machine, but on their own terms or in their own time. They don't hate their creator or its angelic servants, but they have some reason to remain Unchained.<br /> <br /> <i>Once per chapter, the Integrator may defuse a violent situation, causing all aggressors to put down their instruments of violence until the end of the scene (a peace he and his allies can no more break than their opponents), or he may add +3 to a Skill roll to evade, outwit, persuade, or research someone who regards him as an enemy (but not to attack).</i><br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Saboteurs</b> intend to destroy or frustrate the designs of the God-Machine.<br /> <br /> <i>Once per chapter, the Saboteur may suggest or set off a chain of random events that inserts chaos (and possibly an Environmental Tilt) into the current scene (subject to Storyteller approval), or he may add +3 to a Physical Skill roll.</i><br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Tempters</b> seek worldly power, influence, and comforts.<br /> <br /> <i>Once per chapter, the Tempter may choose to receive VIP treatment at a location that isn't actively looking for her or her companions (including conspiracy facilities), or she may add +3 to a Social Skill roll.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Gadgets:</b> The Unchained can imbue mundane objects with their Embeds and Exploits, resulting in a Gadget usable by anyone. The demon may purchase Gadgets based on an Embed/Exploit she has at a cost of 1 Experience \u2014 or 2 Experiences for one she doesn't have.<br /> <br /> Once per chapter, the Unchained may replace one Gadget with another with the same Experiences cost, but this is a compromise (p. XX). Replacing a Gadget causes the existing one to lose its properties or pass to a new owner outside of the cohort.<br /> <br /> <b>Pacts:</b> Demons can enter contracts with anyone who willingly (i.e., without supernatural mental influence being involved) signs them, representing virtually any kind of agreed-upon exchange between the parties. Pacts allow Unchained to create and improve Covers while granting the signatory useful gifts. Negotiating a pact requires at least an hour and is usually resolved using the Social Maneuvering system if the demon needs a specific person to sign who may be hesitant to do so. Assuming he finds a willing signer, the Unchained can forge a pact to accomplish one of the following:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Friendly Pact:</b> The demon crafts a pact primarily to give a temporary benefit to someone \u2014 whether it's an ally or a party she owes a favor. Spend a point of Willpower. Until the end of the chapter, the signee gains up to four dots in a new Social Merit or increases his rating in an existing Social Merit by two. This can't affect Deviant-only Merits or the Fast-Talking Merit.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Commodity:</b> Among the Unchained, pacts are a fungible commodity, and the demon crafts a pact with the intent of transferring it to another of his kind in exchange for a favor. Spend a point of Willpower. Until the end of the chapter, the demon gains up to two dots in a new Merit or increases her rating in an existing Merit by one. Except with Storyteller approval, this can only affect the following Merits: Allies, Contacts, Library, Mentor, Resources, Retainer, Safe Place, Staff, or Status. Multiple pacts can affect the same Merit.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Reserve:</b> Rather than cashing it in or selling it right away, the demon saves it for later. Spend a point of Willpower. The Unchained adds the pact to his reserve and may use it later for the Commodity or Patch Job effect.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Patch Job:</b> This requires the Legend Lesser Form. Spend a point of Willpower. Exchange five pacts for a new Cover or exchange any number of pacts to increase the Unchained's Cover rating by 1 per pact (to a maximum of 10).<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Soul Pact:</b> This requires the Legend Lesser Form. The demon convinces someone to sell himself to her entirely, signing the pact in his own blood. This always requires Social Maneuvering, typically with a large number of Doors; even the most na\u00efve Baseline isn't usually willing to sign one of these without considerable cajoling. If successful, spend a point of Willpower to gain the signer as a new Cover. The demon may instead keep the soul pact in reserve, as above, in which case it counts as five pacts or can be cashed in later for the new Cover.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-09T04:10:27-08:00","id":1556378,"title":"","content":"Remember earlier how I said I always knew mages would be tricky? I was right. However, it wasn't <i>impossible</i>. The Techniques system from Shallow Graves makes it a whole lot easier to emulate improvised magic than could have been done with the core book alone. Moreover, rendering Awakened magic as several separate Lesser Forms allows a Storyteller to pare down the splat to cover a range of lesser sorcerers \u2013 apprentices, hedge wizards, or occult scholars who have learned just enough magic to make themselves a danger to themselves and others.<br /> <br /> Hunters are the splat most likely to befriend or run afoul of a Deviant cohort, and you can read a bit more about them for free at the Patreon. Full text of both splats is available to patrons.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Arcana</span><br /> <br /> Mages divide the power over which they have mastery into 10 Arcana (sing. Arcanum). By default, a mage's rating in an Arcanum is 0, meaning she has no power over it. Awakened who have learned an Arcanum have a rating in it ranging from 1 to 5 dots.<br /> <br /> Whenever the mage gains her first dot in an Arcanum from any of the Lesser Forms, she chooses four Variations within its purview with the approval of the Storyteller. She may cast spells emulating those Variations at Magnitude 1. Variations like Hyper-Competence and Cursed Flesh that are actually collections of related Variations must be selected as individual spells for each specific Variation. Hyper-Competence (Brawl) 1 is a different spell from Hyper-Competence (Persuasion) 1, for example.<br /> <br /> Whenever she increases her Arcanum rating, she increases the maximum Magnitude of these Variations by 1.<br /> <br /> Lash is a special case. A Bruising Lash is only available at Arcanum 3 or higher, and a Cutting Lash is only available at Arcanum 4 or higher. Only Gentle Lashes are available at Arcanum 1 and 2.<br /> <br /> <b>Spells can be used to improvise Techniques, subject to the normal once-per-chapter restriction.</b><br /> <br /> <b>Death:</b> This Arcanum concerns cold, darkness, decay, enervation, ghosts, and souls.<br /> <br /> <b>Fate:</b> This Arcanum includes blessings, destiny, fortune, hexes, oaths, and probability.<br /> <br /> <b>Forces:</b> This Arcanum commands electricity, fire, gravity, heat, light, movement, sound, and weather.<br /> <br /> <b>Life:</b> This is the Arcanum of animals, disease, food, healing, metamorphosis, Physical Attributes and actions, and plants.<br /> <br /> <b>Matter:</b> This is the magic of alchemy, crafting, gases, liquids, shaping, solids, stasis and transmutation.<br /> <br /> <b>Mind:</b> This Arcanum concerns communication, dreams, emotion, hallucination, language, memory, Mental and Social Attributes and actions, mental projection, and thought.<br /> <br /> <b>Prime:</b> This Arcanum commands Awakened magic, illusion, Mana, and truth.<br /> <br /> <b>Space:</b> This is the magic of conjuration, distance, names, scrying, separation, sympathy, and warding.<br /> <br /> <b>Spirit:</b> This Arcanum concerns animistic spirits and otherworlds.<br /> <br /> <b>Time:</b> This is the magic of prophecy, change, postcognition, prophecy, time contraction and dilation, and time travel.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Awakened Magic</span><br /> <br /> The magic of the Wise involves the mage imposing his will on reality to make his vision (what the Awakened call the <i>imago</i>) real. Only a rare few accomplish this in the first place, and it takes long practice to sustain the <i>imago</i> for more than a few minutes at a time.<br /> <br /> By default, a spell has the following properties:<br /> <br /> \u2022 Casting a spell requires an instant action and a successful roll using the mage's Gnosis (minimum 1) + dots in the Arcanum of the spell. The mage must choose to either release or contain the Paradox the spell generates before making this roll (see below).<br /> <br /> \u2022 The spell hits one subject of Size 5 or less (i.e., valid targets), or an area equal to an arm's-length circle around a point.<br /> <br /> \u2022 The mage must be touching the subject of the spell, or be casting on herself, or must make a successful Gnosis + Athletics/Firearms \u2013 Defense attack with a range of 10/20/40.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Instead of wielding a non-Directed spell himself, a mage may choose to grant it to a target as with Sacred Flesh (Boon), allowing them to enjoy the benefits as though they had the Variation during the duration. The benefits last until the end of the scene (or until they are dispelled, or the mage dismisses them as a reflexive action) and count the mage's Scar Power as 1. Directed Variations and effects (including a ranged Lash) can only be wielded by the mage, not granted.<br /> <br /> \u2022 An unwilling or unknowing sapient creature can't be targeted if their relevant Resistance Attribute exceeds the mage's Arcanum rating + 1.<br /> <br /> \u2022 If called upon to make a Scar Finesse roll to exercise exacting control over a Variation, the willworker uses Gnosis (minimum 1) + Arcanum rating.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Reach and Grasp</span><br /> <br /> A mage's <b>Grasp</b> represents the safe limits of his magic. When casting a spell, a mage's Grasp is equal to his Arcanum rating. A spellcaster can increase his Grasp by 1 for a given spell by undertaking a ritual lasting at least an hour.<br /> <br /> Every spell has a <b>Reach</b> based on how difficult it is to cast. By default, a spell's Reach is equal to the Arcanum rating (i.e., Variation Magnitude) at which it is being cast. A mage who wishes to overcome the usual limits of her magic can do so by increasing its Reach. Some of these parameters we'll describe later, but some are universal to all Awakened:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Range (+1 Reach):</b> The spell can be targeted at sensory range.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Potency (Variable):</b> Increase Scar Power and Reach on a one-for-one basis.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Spell Capacity (Variable):</b> If the mage already has active spells, add Reach equal to the number of active spells.<br /> <br /> As long as a spell's Reach doesn't exceed the mage's Grasp, it remains fully within her control. If it exceeds her Grasp, she risks Paradox.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Paradox</span><br /> <br /> Paradox is the fruit of hubris. If a spell's Reach exceeds the mage's Grasp, he must choose either to release or attempt to contain the Paradox. Either way, the Storyteller rolls the spell's total Reach (not just the amount by which it exceeded his Grasp) as a dice pool (the Paradox roll):<br /> <br /> \u2022 If the Paradox is <b>released</b>, each success imposes a Persistent Scar of a Magnitude equal to the Arcanum rating of the spell on someone in the scene of the Storyteller's choosing (other than the mage). This lasts until the end of the chapter (until the end of the scene for non-Baselines), although the Storyteller may extend it until the end of the story (or end of chapter for non-Baselines) or make it permanent (or end of story for non-Baselines) by spending an additional one or two successes respectively. If the Paradox roll achieves more successes than the spellcasting roll, the Storyteller chooses whether the spell fails outright or succeeds but creates a significant complication.<br /> <br /> \u2022 If the mage attempts to <b>contain</b> the Paradox, he instead contests the Paradox pool with his Wisdom (or one die if he lacks the Mage Greater Form). For this spellcasting, the mage suffers a Controlled Scar of the Storyteller's choosing with a Magnitude equal to the successes on the Paradox roll in excess of those on the Wisdom roll (to a maximum of Magnitude 5). If the Paradox roll achieves more successes than the spellcasting roll, the Storyteller chooses whether the spell fails outright or succeeds but imposes a Persistent Scar of her choice on the mage; this Scar has a Magnitude equal to the Arcanum rating of the spell and lasts until the end of the chapter.<br /> <br /> \u2022 The Scars and complications produced by Paradox rolls should reflect the Arcanum of the spell and the mage's intent or state of mind. The player may make suggestions, but the Storyteller is the final arbiter.<br /> <br /> <b>Dissonance:</b> Baseline witnesses erode Awakened magic. If the spell is Subtle, reduce the spell's total Reach by 1 at the end of each chapter it (or its target) was in the same scene as a Baseline; this diminishes a spell factor of the Storyteller's choice. If a spell's Reach is reduced to less than zero, it is dispelled. If it was Subtle but shared the scene with many Baselines, or if it was Overt, apply this at the end of each <i>scene</i>. If it was Overt and many Baselines witnessed it, apply this at the end of each <i>minute</i> (each turn in action scenes). Baselines rationalize or forget any Overt magic they witness, although they still suffer a breaking point.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-11T04:01:05-08:00","id":1556466,"title":"","content":"I think it's fair to say that Mummy and Promethean are weird games in that they include some quite experimental mechanics and gameplay loops. Mummy doesn't believe in linear character advancement (or time). Modeling the variability of Roles and Refinements in the space allotted was an interesting challenge, and I'm sharing the result of that today.<br /> <br /> You can also suffer Sybaris by witnessing the awe-inspiring miracles called Utterances, which are available for free at the Patreon (and the full text of both splats is available to patrons).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Transmutations</span><br /> <br /> Transmutations are supernatural powers granted by the Refinement Lesser Form (p. XX), transforming a Promethean's humours to yield potent tools for her Pilgrimage. Each Transmutation comprises four-tiered <i>Alembics</i>, Scar-free Variations that express the Transmutation's alchemical principle:<br /> <br /> \u2022 The first tier is a Persistent Magnitude 1 Variation. If Toggled, it gains the Reflexive keyword.<br /> <br /> \u2022 The second tier is a Magnitude 2 Variation usable once per chapter. If Perpetual, it becomes Toggled.<br /> <br /> \u2022 The third tier is a Magnitude 3 Variation usable only once per chapter. If an Adaptation is used to reset this Variation, it inflicts a minor Instability each time after the first that it's used during the chapter. If a character has the Promethean Greater Template, she must instead spend 1 Pyros on top of the usual reset cost. If Perpetual, it becomes Toggled.<br /> <br /> \u2022 The fourth tier is a Magnitude 4 Variation usable only once per story, with each additional use in the same story inflicting a medium Instability (or costing 3 Pyros, if the character has the Greater Form). If Perpetual, it becomes Toggled.<br /> <br /> Alembics come in many forms, and the player and Storyteller can determine which Variations work best with each character's Transmutation and Roles; each Transmutation's general principles are noted below.<br /> <br /> <b>Alchemicus: </b>Internal and external innovation through alchemy (e.g., Matter Control \u2014 <b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 95).<br /> <br /> <b>Benefice: </b>Fostering community and connection (e.g., Refuge \u2014 <b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. XX).<br /> <br /> <b>Contamination: </b>Finding, purging, or encouraging impurities (e.g., Deadly Ichor).<br /> <br /> <b>Corporeum: </b>Achievingphysical perfection (e.g., Superhuman [Attribute]).<br /> <br /> <b>Deception: </b>Blending in with (or hiding from) Baselines (e.g., Mimicry).<br /> <br /> <b>Disquietism: </b>Redirecting or inflicting Disquiet (e.g., Creeping Dread).<br /> <br /> <b>Electrification: </b>Controlling electricity and machines (e.g., Electrokinesis).<br /> <br /> <b>Luciferus: </b>Inspiration and light manipulation (e.g., Blessing).<br /> <br /> <b>Mesmerism: </b>Mental and emotional control over others (e.g., Pheromones).<br /> <br /> <b>Metamorphosis: </b>Survival and bodily transformation (e.g., Environmental Adaptation).<br /> <br /> <b>Saturninus: </b>Clues and omens (e.g., Shadows of the Past).<br /> <br /> <b>Sensorium: </b>Enhanced perception (e.g., Sensor Array).<br /> <br /> <b>Spiritus: </b>Interactions with supernatural creatures (e.g., Fate's Agent).<br /> <br /> <b>Vitality: </b>Physical and mental fortitude (e.g., Carapace).<br /> <br /> <b>Vulcanus: </b>Control over fire and Pyros (e.g., Pyrokinesis).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Lesser Form: Refinement</span><br /> <br /> Prometheans walk the Pilgrimage through ten Refinements, philosophies that offer insights into the human condition and alter their humours.<br /> <br /> <b>Strange Alchemies: </b>At character creation, choose a Refinement:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Aes: </b>The Refinement of Bronze \u2014 Aid. Sentries seek to live their lives for others to better understand human kindness and compassion. <b>Transmutations: </b>Benefice, Corporeum. <b>Roles: </b>Bodyguard, Servant, Seeker.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Argentum: </b>The Refinement of Silver \u2014 Mystery. The Mystics seek to understand the boundaries of the human condition by studying other supernatural creatures. <b>Transmutations:</b> Sensorium, Spiritus. <b>Roles: </b>Envoy, Observer, Warden.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Aurum: </b>The Refinement of Gold \u2014 Mortality. The Mimics seek to make themselves mirrors of humanity. <b>Transmutations: </b>Deception, Mesmerism. <b>Roles: </b>Companion, Follower, Leader.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Cobalus: </b>The Refinement of Cobalt \u2014 Impurity. The Cathars seek human sin and imperfection so they can identify it in themselves. <b>Transmutations: </b>Contamination, Mesmerism. <b>Roles: </b>Confessor, Deviant, Provocateur.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Cuprum: </b>The Refinement of Copper \u2014 Self. The Pariahs seek to learn the power of solitude and prevent themselves from hurting those they hold dear. <b>Transmutations: </b>Metamorphosis, Sensorium. <b>Roles: </b>Hermit, Sage, Watcher.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Ferrum: </b>The Refinement of Iron \u2014 Corpus. The Titans seek to perfect the physical form in preparation for spiritual transformation. <b>Transmutations: </b>Corporeum, Vitality. <b>Roles: </b>Exemplar, Martyr, Soldier.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Mercurius: </b>The Refinement of Quicksilver \u2014 Pyros. The Savants seek to study alchemy to better facilitate their transition from lead into gold. <b>Transmutations: </b>Alchemicus, Vulcanus. <b>Roles: </b>Craftsman, Explorer, Scientist.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Phosphorum: </b>The Refinement of Phosphorous \u2014 Ephemerality. The Light-Bringers seek to understand the fleetingness of life by running headlong into mortal danger. <b>Transmutations: </b>Luciferus, Vulcanus. <b>Roles: </b>Daredevil, Psychopomp, Whip.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Plumbum: </b>The Refinement of Lead \u2014 Source. The Originists seek to understand the Promethean condition before they move on. <b>Transmutations: </b>Disquietism, Saturninus. <b>Roles: </b>Chronicler, Ascetic, Pilgrim.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Stannum: </b>The Refinement of Tin \u2014 Torment. The Furies seek to understand their personal Torment, channeling their pain in useful directions. <b>Transmutations: </b>Electrification, Disquietism. <b>Roles: </b>Outcast, Savage, Vigilante.<br /> <br /> <b>Role:</b> Each Role is associated with one Alembic of each of its Refinement's favored Transmutations (chosen at character creation or the first time the Promethean assumes a new Refinement/Role). Whenever the Created acts in accordance with her current Role and it creates a complication, particularly one that teaches her about humanity, she takes a Beat. The Promethean can change her Role (but not her Refinement) once per chapter; this takes at least an hour.<br /> <br /> <b>Calcification:</b> The Promethean may calcify an Alembic, granting permanent access to it even when he is in a different Refinement/Role.<br /> <br /> This Lesser Form grants a single Refinement with three Roles (each with one Alembic of each favored Transmutation) but no calcified Alembics. Calcified Alembics cost 2 Experiences each.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Lesser Form: Walking the Path</span><br /> <br /> <b>Prerequisites: </b>Refinement Lesser Form<br /> <br /> Prometheans have a better chance of achieving the New Dawn by exploring and mastering different Refinements.<br /> <br /> <b>Changing Refinements:</b> The Created is no longer limited to only changing Roles within his chosen Refinement. He may instead change Refinements but is still limited to changing Role/Refinement once per chapter.<br /> <br /> <b>Affinity Refinements:</b> The Promethean favors certain Refinements over others \u2014 his Affinity Refinements. Changing Refinements to one of the Created's Affinity Refinements does not count against the per-chapter limit. He may adopt non-Affinity Refinements, but these count against the per-chapter limit.<br /> <br /> <b>Torment Affinity:</b> The Promethean automatically treats the Refinement of Tin as an Affinity Refinement and can change to it as a reflexive action.<br /> <br /> This Lesser Form grants four Affinity Refinements \u2014 the Created's starting Refinement and three others of his choice. Additional Affinity Refinements cost 2 Experiences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-13T04:01:46-08:00","id":1556550,"title":"","content":"It might surprise you to learn that I\u2019ve never really had much interest in vampires. Coming from someone who has as many urban fantasy/horror writing credits as I\u2019ve accumulated over that last 17 years, that seems like an almost shameful admission, but it\u2019s true. Which is why I decided to bring Sam Young \u2013 who has produced more words about Requiem vampires than just about anyone out there \u2013 to write that section of Black Vans. It was definitely the right call, and I\u2019m sharing some of what he came up with today.<br /> <br /> He also worked on Promethean and Werewolf, the latter of which has some spoilers available for free at the Patreon (and patrons can see both splats\u2019 full text, as always).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Lesser Form: Properties of the Blood</span><br /> <br /> <b>Blood Bonds: </b>Once per chapter, if a human or Remade drinks a vampire\u2019s blood, no matter how much, that mortal risks gaining an emotional bond called a Vinculum. This works like the Cursed Flesh (Secretion; Long-Lasting) Variation (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 109), bestowing the Fixation Scar (p. XX) toward the source of the Vitae and imposing the Addicted Condition (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 320) for the Kindred\u2019s blood. The first drink imposes Fixation 1, the second Fixation 2, and the third Fixation 3 (Devout). A third-step bond breaks any other Vincula and renders the character immune to them until it ends.<br /> <br /> <b>Physical Intensity: </b>Once per scene, a vampire can increase a Physical Attribute by 1 (but not derived traits) as a reflexive action. This lasts until the end of the scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Fangs: </b>Vampires have retractable fangs that deal +0L damage (which can\u2019t be healed more quickly by occult means) and may feed (p. XX). If the character lacks the Vampire Greater Form, if she would gain Vitae, she instead resets a per-scene or per-chapter power of her choice but may not do so more than once per scene.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Lesser Form: Tricks of the Damned</span><br /> <br /> <b>Dead Physiology: </b>A Vampire is an ambulatory corpse. While sleeping or in torpor, she appears to be dead.<br /> <br /> \u2022 She enjoys the benefits of Anomalous Biology 5 and downgrades all non-supernatural lethal and aggravated damage by a step.<br /> <br /> \u2022 She cannot eat, drink, copulate, or perform other bodily functions (although she can speak and move), which can give away her true nature. Once per chapter, she may suppress these restrictions and appear completely alive until the end of the scene.<br /> <br /> \u2022 If she suffers lethal damage in her right-most Health box, instead of dying, she falls into torpor at the end of the turn, awakening at the end of the chapter (but see exceptions under Humanity, above) or when fed Vitae from a character with the Vampire Greater Template.<br /> <br /> \u2022 If she suffers at least five damage from a wooden stake that successfully targets her heart, she enters torpor until it\u2019s removed.<br /> <br /> <b>Heightened Senses: </b>Vampires can sense the presence of blood, heartbeats, and other vampires within 10 yards/meters. Supernatural concealment provokes a Clash of Wills using the vampire\u2019s Wits + Blood Potency (or Acclimation).<br /> <br /> <b>The Lost Visage: </b>Unless she chooses to, a vampire never appears clearly or directly in mirrors, photographs, video feeds, etc., with some coincidence or technical failure always obscuring her identifying features. This effect is supernatural in nature but mundane in outcome: The vampire\u2019s face isn\u2019t hidden by magic; it just isn\u2019t captured.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Scar Form: The Curse</span><br /> <br /> A character with this Lesser Form has unentangled Persistent Scars of Magnitude 3 (or Magnitude based on Blood Potency and Humanity, if she has the Vampire Greater Form) as follows:<br /> <br /> <b>Hemovore: </b>A vampire must drink blood to survive, as Dependency (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 162).<br /> <br /> <b>Daysleep: </b>Vampires must rest during daylight hours, as the Nocturnal Scar (p. XX).<br /> <br /> <b>Fire and Sunlight:</b> Vampires burn in sunlight per the Bane (Common) Scar, and fire always inflicts aggravated damage (which can\u2019t be downgraded).<br /> <br /> <b>Frenzy: </b>Kindred can usually keep their bestial urges in check, but sometimes these compulsions overwhelm them. All vampires bear the Murderous Urge 1 and Phobia 1 Scars. The character may reset a per-scene or per-chapter Variation by increasing the Magnitude of either Scar by 2 until the end of the scene but can\u2019t do so if its Magnitude is already 5.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">New Persistent Scar: Fixation (\u2022 to \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022)</span><br /> <br /> <b>Keywords:</b> Social; Subtle<br /> <br /> The Deviant is consumed by an obsession with a person chosen at the time she develops this Scar.<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022, the Remade obsesses over a Baseline. The object of this fixation cannot be a Loyalty Touchstone. If he later becomes a Loyalty Touchstone, increase the Magnitude of this Scar by 1 until he is no longer both a Loyalty Touchstone and an object of her Fixation. Her player may accept a Beat whenever interacting with or fixating on that character would create a complication.<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022\u2022, as Magnitude \u2022, and the Broken suffers the Swooned Condition (p. XX) towards the object of her fixation. If resolved, this Condition reestablishes itself at the beginning of the next chapter.<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022, as Magnitude \u2022\u2022, and choose one:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Devout: </b>If the Deviant\u2019s intended action or inaction regarding the object of her fixation would be considered Faltering if he were a Loyalty Touchstone, the Remade cannot act (or fail to act) in that way unless she makes a successful Scar Resistance roll. The Storyteller may invoke this once per chapter.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Fickle:</b> Once per chapter, the Storyteller may change the object of the Broken\u2019s fixation to another person she doesn\u2019t know well but who has recently had close personal contact with her, such as someone she has fed from.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Polycule: </b>The Deviant has additional objects of fixation equal to 4 \u2013 half Scar Resistance (rounded up).<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022, two Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022 effects apply.<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022, all Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022 effects apply.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">New Persistent Scar: Nocturnal (\u2022 to \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022)</span><br /> <br /> <b>Keywords:</b> Physical; Subtle<br /> <br /> The Deviant\u2019s day-night cycle is reversed.<br /> <br /> The Remade can operate during the daylight hours if she must, but it\u2019s unpleasant, and no matter what she does, she can\u2019t seem to reset to the Baseline default.<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022, she takes a Beat when resting during the day and waking at night creates a complication for her cohort.<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022\u2022, as Magnitude \u2022, and the Remade loses the 10-again quality on mundane actions during daylight hours.<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022, as Magnitude \u2022\u2022, and choose one:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Lethargic: </b>The Deviant suffers the Fatigued Condition during daylight hours, beginning immediately at dawn. The \u20131 modifier on actions worsens every three hours instead of every six (to a maximum of \u20135). The accumulated penalty is suppressed during nighttime hours, but resumes at sunrise and continues to accumulate. If the Broken sleeps at least six hours during the daytime, the Condition resolves.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Spent: </b>The Deviant can\u2019t spend Willpower on mundane actions (or to increase Resistance traits) during daylight hours.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Stupefied: </b>The Deviant\u2019s Scar Finesse rolls during daylight hours suffer a penalty equal to 4 \u2013 half Scar Resistance (rounded up).<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022, two Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022 effects apply.<br /> <br /> At Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022, all Magnitude \u2022\u2022\u2022 effects apply.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-16T04:01:54-08:00","id":1556706,"title":"","content":"The final chapter of Black Vans takes Deviant out of its modern horror setting and into other genres, sketching out new settings and altering several of the standard systems to convey them. It's the longest single chapter \u2013 a seven-page general section and then six sections of 12-14 pages each. In addition to each of these being (in my opinion) pretty cool on their own, they're meant to serve as platforms on which Storytellers can build their own, mixing and matching systems to produce a genre that will best tell their troupe's Deviant stories. In several cases, I could have used the same system for multiple genres but opted to create two different ones specifically so that troupes would have more options.<br /> <br /> Today I'm sharing an excerpt from the Cyberpunk setting that focuses on the world-building elements of the genre. The free preview at the Patreon showcases the Touchstone alternative the genre uses (with the full text of the intro and the Cyberpunk genre available to patrons).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Information Warfare</span><br /> <br /> While many terms in <b>Deviant: The Renegades</b> are perfectly serviceable, some have negative connotations world powers would prefer to avoid inserting into public awareness. They've deprecated these terms and replaced them with more marketable ones. Oftentimes, they've kept more acceptable terms for the same thing.<br /> <br /> <b>Divergence:</b> This term has fallen out of use. While humans are still capable of Divergence in the traditional sense, it's rarer than ever before, and few scientists have any interest in the crude methodologies of the past. Advertisers instead focus on and promote product groups called <b>Platforms</b>. When speaking about the process more generally, the most common terms are variations on the words &quot;Upgrade&quot;, &quot;Alter&quot;, &quot;modify&quot;, &quot;improve&quot;, and &quot;transform&quot;.<br /> <br /> <b>Clade:</b> &quot;Platform&quot; refers to the products that grant Variations (<b>Upgrades</b>), rather than to those who have them, but given the problems that often arise when mixing Upgrades of different Platforms, it has become nearly synonymous with the traditional term &quot;Clade&quot;.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Cephalist:</b> &quot;Psychic&quot; remains the dominant general term, but earlier generations of influential Cephalist Devoted have cemented the terms &quot;Biokinetic&quot;, &quot;Clairsentient&quot;, &quot;Pyschokinetic&quot;, and &quot;Telepath&quot; into the popular lexicon. Today's Progenitors install psychic powers using neurosurgery, patented cocktails of hallucinogens and anesthetics, or through classes or rituals prefaced by dire NDAs. Upgrades administered in this way are typically described as <b>Gifts</b>.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Chimeric:</b> Despite efforts to coin a new term like &quot;Host&quot; or &quot;Recipient&quot; \u2014 as well as a desperate attempt to elevate &quot;Hybrid&quot; \u2014 the one that has stuck is &quot;Splicer&quot;. The transplantation of vat-grown organs, limbs, and tissue is the most common approach when working on this Platform, although injections of bioengineered organisms are poised to overtake such inelegant methodologies within the next decade. Upgrades administered in this way are typically called <b>Splices</b>.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Coactive:</b> &quot;Infused&quot; and &quot;Vessels&quot; are the most common terms for those with Upgrades of this Platform. Once the province of mystical conspiracies, mastery of the principles on which these Divergences operate has rendered the physics and physiology of them less arcane. A clinic that services Infused requires careful consideration of its location \u2014 proximity to an appropriate otherworldly portal, favorable geomantic properties, or a hardened electrical grid and backups to prevent the holding tank of captured spirits from escaping. Upgrades of this Platform are typically called <b>Infusions</b>.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Invasive</b>: &quot;Cyborg&quot; is the overwhelmingly dominant term, although some insist on calling both the Platform's Upgrades and recipients &quot;Bionics&quot;. For the most part, installation matches the popular depiction of cybernetics in 20th century science fiction \u2014 surgical replacement of living tissue with machines and the implantation of microchips and other tiny electronic devices under the skin. Installing occult artifacts has proved more difficult, although engineers are working on it. Upgrades of this Platform are called cybernetic implants, which most render either as <b>Cybernetics</b> or <b>Implants</b>.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Mutant:</b> The official market-speak for a recipient of Upgrades of this Platform is &quot;Artisanal&quot;, but most of those in the biz call them &quot;Customs&quot;. Both evoke the personal touch this Platform's Upgrades require. Careful screening is a necessary step that allows an organization to personalize the Upgrade to the recipient's precise genetic sequence, ensuring that the injection that comes at the end of the process will result in gene-deep modification of the customer, rather than deadly anaphylactic response. Mutant Upgrades are known as <b>Therapies</b>.<br /> <br /> <b>Deviant:</b> As a general term for those who have had Upgrades installed in them, &quot;Deviant&quot; is deeply deprecated, and &quot;Broken&quot; is unthinkable. &quot;Remade&quot; lingers on, but &quot;Upgraded&quot; is more common, and &quot;Altered&quot;, &quot;modified&quot;, and &quot;transformed&quot; also enjoy regular use. The Upgraded of the present age are hardly recognizable as the technological descendants of yesterday's Broken. The process is no longer random, and while rare installation complications are possible, they're seldom fatal. Moreover, manufacturers have the means to reverse unwanted transformations, although Upgrades are expensive enough to install that few lay out the additional capital for Downgrades (no manufacturer offers money-back guarantees that cover those installation costs, even if a few are willing to refund the cost of recently installed Upgrades).<br /> <br /> <b>Variations:</b> The most common catch-all term for Variations is &quot;Upgrades&quot;, although &quot;modifications&quot;, &quot;Alterations&quot;, and &quot;improvements&quot; all see use. Other terms are largely Platform-specific (see above). Like Variations, Upgrades have limitations or side-effects. Unlike them, these side-effects generally don't worsen unless the Remade has a new Upgrade installed or replaces an existing Upgrade with a more powerful version.<br /> <br /> <b>Touchstone:</b> The installation of Upgrades doesn't involve soul-shattering trauma that blasts away the recipient's sense of self. The Altered don't require or have Loyalty or Conviction Touchstones. Nothing prevents them from deriving satisfaction from little indulgences and grand gestures. In practice, however, the Upgrades are an expensive investment that places the transformed deep into debt, and merely paying the interest on those debts is an undertaking that demands most of the Altered's time and energy. Instead of Touchstones, Upgraded have <b>Sponsors</b> and <b>Patrons</b> (collectively known as <b>collectives</b>), each of which has a <b>Figurehead</b> who serves as the Remade's primary contact with the Sponsor/Patron as a collective group.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Sponsors:</b> These are collectives that expect or demand that the Upgraded perform work for them. Most often they are an employer who knows very well what modifications the Altered has and fully expects her to use them in the company's service. Others are those who bankrolled the installation of one or more modifications \u2014 be it a government agency, lender, former employer, or rich relative. Job assignments from a Sponsor are usually issued with clearly delineated objectives and rules of engagement. Refusing them has consequences \u2014 and failure is frowned upon \u2014 but as long as the Remade made a bona fide effort to complete the mission a Sponsor is still inclined to pay her. Sponsors more closely resemble Loyalty Touchstones.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Patrons:</b> These are collectives to whom the Upgrade <i>owes</i> nothing but who will reward the Remade's service with money or future favors. They can include gig employers who pay handsomely for contract work. Rescuing strangers from street toughs or stealing from the super-rich and giving the proceeds to charity can win friends who turn out to have valuable connections and unexpected resources. Job assignments from Patrons are usually more flexible in how the Altered carries them out, but they tend to pay poorly unless the transformed is wildly successful. Patrons more closely resemble Conviction Touchstones.<br /> <br /> <b>Conspiracy:</b> The conspiracies that once defined the Web of Pain are a thing of the past. Most are now publicly traded companies and conglomerates that openly advertise their products to potential consumers. A minority are government agencies that closely guard the secrets of their Upgrades for national security reasons. Most of the cults and fringe religions that dabbled in the Divergence never found a way to mass-produce Deviants and are rapidly dying out. People generally want a predictable transformation, not one subject to the whims of chance and as likely to kill them as to Upgrade them. The Remade instead find themselves entangled in a <b>Network</b> of Sponsors and Patrons that make bids for the cohort's time and talent.<br /> <br /> <b>Instability:</b> The changes wrought by installing Upgrades don't make the transformed prone to disintegration. Instead, the Stability track becomes <b>Credit</b> and measures her capacity to endure <b>Pressure</b>, which is the sum of debts owed to various interested parties. Increased Pressure doesn't affect the side-effects of the Altered's Upgrades (her Scars), but nor is it a pathway to increased occult power. If the rightmost box of a character's Credit track is filled with major Pressure, her creditors have decided to remotely disable (or forcibly repossess) her Upgrades, which they successfully do at the end of the current chapter. This form of End Stage doesn't supercharge the modified's Upgrades, but its only cure remains successfully completing a mission for a Patron, which represents a big score that wipes out a crucial debt.<br /> <br /> <b>Devoted vs. Renegade:</b> Although their mechanics are identical, current parlance is <b>Suits</b> vs. <b>Freelancers</b>. Suits are those who do more work for Sponsors than for Patrons, while Freelancers are the opposite. Upgraded can switch &quot;sides&quot; once per story and may do so even if they have the &quot;wrong&quot; ratio of Sponsors to Patrons (i.e., a Suit with more Patrons than Sponsors or a Freelancer with more Sponsors than Patrons).<br /> <br /> <b>Icon:</b> Although this term is commonly used, it means something very different in this genre. An Icon is a user's virtual self or avatar, which exists simultaneously with the user's physical body. It can assail (and be assailed by) rival Icons and can often interact with machines and Upgrades to influence the material world (often rendered as <b>Realspace</b>). <b>Iconspace</b> is parlance for the virtual world that overlays our own, which often serves as a battlefield between Icons.<br /> <br /> <b>Node:</b> Like Icon, this term carries a different meaning in this genre. A node is a data access point that permits rapid Iconspace travel between Realspace points or that serves as a gateway into an artificial environment in Iconspace that has no corresponding location in Realspace.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-18T03:20:31-08:00","id":1556785,"title":"","content":"Deviant was a late arrival to the Chronicles of Darkness \u2013 so late, in fact, that it still wasn't released until all the Dark Eras books were published. Dark Eras 2 has a couple of settings that feature Remade, but the Broken are rarely mentioned outside of them. The Dark Eras genre of Black Vans attempts to rectify that by discussing what the lives of Deviants were like before modern times, a portion of which is what I'm previewing today. It also provides dozens of examples of Divergence methodologies that have been used throughout history, some of which are the bonus free preview for today at the Patreon. And as always, patrons have access to the full text.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Broken Roles</span><br /> <br /> Throughout history, Deviants' Variations and Scars, taken together with the various side-effects of their damaged souls, have predisposed them to certain societal roles. In some cases, a given sentiment may only apply to individual Remade, based on their abilities, appearances, and behavior. After all, most people don't recognize Deviants as a single supernatural phenomenon, since Broken are so varied. However, in communities that have witnessed the violent rampage of a Deviant in End Stage, or where a family of Broken have long been pillars of society, anyone even suspected of being a Deviant may be treated the same way \u2014 for better or worse. Deviants who leave a familiar community for one where they're strangers might avoid the stigma of being Remade, but they can face suspicion, especially if they can't adequately explain why they would have left their birthplace.<br /> <br /> <b>Criminal:</b> The Broken operates outside the law, using their powers to give them an advantage when perpetrating crimes and to avoid apprehension. They might be driven to this by necessity, stealing or selling illicit goods or services to make ends meet because they have no other viable options (at least none that aren't worse). They may instead commit crimes because it's an easy way to make a lot of money quickly, and they've grown accustomed to living lavishly. Or they could even commit crimes in the service of a higher purpose \u2014 using the money to help the poor, avenging the victims of those who think they are out of reach, or working to dismantle corrupt organizations and systems (violently, if necessary).<br /> <br /> <b>Cryptid:</b> The Deviant is a local legend who lives in a remote place just beyond the edge of civilization, often among others like her. She rarely shows herself, but stories about her endure \u2014 some of them told to frighten children, and others to fill them with wonder. Most visitors assume the tales are superstition, or at least wildly exaggerated.<br /> <br /> <b>Figurehead:</b> The Remade appears to have a position of power and prominence but is only a figurehead for powers behind the throne. He could be hailed as a god by an order of priests who use his supernatural abilities to keep their cultists obedient. He might be a monarch whose decrees are carefully guided by scheming viziers and bureaucrats. Or he may be the heir to a fortune or other family legacy coveted by his parents' retainers or by the scions of other wealthy families. In many cases, the Broken's Variations aren't the attraction. It's the Deviant's vulnerability to manipulation by those he trusts that these jackals find so appealing.<br /> <br /> <b>Freak:</b> The Broken is an object of curiosity rarely treated as a human being. Whether his audience consists of doctors and scientists or the patrons of a carnival or specialty brothel, the Remade is a source of entertainment. His handlers routinely dehumanize him and discourage him from seeking a life outside of their control, whether they do so out of cruelty and avarice or a genuine if misguided desire to protect him.<br /> <br /> <b>Hero:</b> The Deviant's powers command the respect of most Baselines (even those who don't like her personally or politically), and children idolize her. The Broken might routinely hunt and destroy monsters, playing a valuable role in society that makes people more forgiving of her failings. Or she might perform other services for her community. For the most part, the Broken is allowed to live as she likes, although the Baseline populace might provide her with material support. If the community grows too dependent on the Remade it can cause a shift in her social role.<br /> <br /> <b>Monster:</b> The Remade is regarded as nothing less than a monster. People fear him at best and organize themselves to destroy him at worst (whether as groups of dedicated monster hunters or an angry and desperate mob). Those who associate with the Deviant (such as Loyalty Touchstones) are considered traitors and dealt with harshly. Even societies that were once ignorant of the Remade's nature or favorable to him can come to regard him as a monster if he acts monstrously or if he reminds them too much of a Deviant who caused trouble for them in the past.<br /> <br /> <b>Mystic:</b> People in the community believe the Deviant possesses secret knowledge. Most keep their distance \u2014 often out of a mix of superstition, fear, and discomfort at what the Remade represents. Some seek her out for information or advice or beg her for blessings and gifts to aid them in achieving something otherwise impossible. The Broken might live within or on the outskirts of her community \u2014 likely with her Loyalty Touchstones. However, her lifestyle may approach that of a hermit as long as she has Touchstones to whom she can connect even in her isolation.<br /> <br /> <b>Outcast:</b> The Deviant is unwelcome, whether as a consequence of his past behavior (before or after Divergence) or because of how members of the community suspect he will behave. In some cases, this is pure superstition. Remade are often regarded as creatures of ill omen or as useful scapegoats when misfortune strikes. Scars frequently play a role, as well. Many people can't help staring at or subtly avoiding even Baselines with deformities or distinctive appearances, and Broken with visible Scars are no less likely to be the object of these behaviors. Mental and Social Scars can engender characteristics that are just as off-putting.<br /> <br /> <b>Slave:</b> The Broken is completely subjugated by someone who rules over her with threats and punishments, often coupled with praise and rewards \u2014 but never a sincere offer of freedom. She may have been born into slavery or forced into servitude later. Her master demands that she uses her supernatural abilities as he directs and will not allow her to do anything he doesn't wish her to do. He also takes steps to make escape seem impossible.<br /> <br /> <b>Soldier:</b> The Deviant belongs to a military unit. This might be an elite unit of Broken who carry out special operations, or they might serve as a specialist or leader in a squad or company of rank-and-file soldiers. Perhaps they had their powers before they joined up (or were conscripted), or perhaps they were Remade as a reward, a punishment, or an experiment intended to produce a super soldier.<br /> <br /> <b>Tyrant:</b> The Remade uses her Variations (or the threat of them) to subjugate people. Likely, most of her subjects are Baselines, but an especially powerful or charismatic Deviant might command the obedience of other Broken, as well. Remade can much more easily dominate a small community (a family, village, cult, or gang). Large domains make tempting targets for rivals (and avaricious allies), and the occult powers the Broken requires to hold her territory come with Scars that make her vulnerable in ways that seldom trouble Baseline autocrats.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-20T04:00:19-08:00","id":1556853,"title":"","content":"As someone who grew up playing D&amp;D and reading every fantasy series I could find at my local library \u2013 and who spent half of my teens, all of my 20s, and a fair chunk of my 30s co-authoring fantasy novels with my best friend \u2013 I have had a <i>lot</i> of exposure to the genre. So, wedding the genre to Deviant was always going to happen and it was just a matter of exactly which sub-genre would work the best. Although a darker fantasy could have worked, I felt it would retread too much of the Dark Era genre. I ultimately settled on high fantasy for its abundance of non-human inhabitants (which conveniently translate well into the alien species of space opera, making those mechanics reusable), meddling gods (which stand in for conspiracies in this genre), and sense of clear good and evil (which makes a surprisingly good adaptation of Conviction and Loyalty systems). Today I'm sharing the broad shape of the genre's heroes in narrative terms.<br /> <br /> The second free preview at the <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon </a>showcases the genre's sample fantasy species (and the full text is available to patrons).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Heroes</span><br /> <br /> While &quot;cohort&quot; still sees use as a collective noun for the players' characters in this genre, &quot;Deviant&quot;, &quot;Remade&quot;, &quot;Broken&quot;, and &quot;transformed&quot; don't describe them. Instead, they are referred to as <b>heroes</b> in-world and <b>protagonists</b> within this section.<br /> <br /> Those who have gifts like heroes but have turned them to selfish or evil ends are <b>villains</b>. Inhuman creatures or beings that present a threat to the heroes' interests are <b>monsters</b>. Collectively, they are the chronicle's <b>antagonists</b>.<br /> <br /> <b>Origin:</b> Heroes come into their power by many paths, all of which fall into one or more of the categories Origin represents in <b>Deviant</b>. However, this does not shape the protagonists to the degree that it does Deviants. Instead, a hero's Origin reflects a combination of <b>Heritage</b> and <b>Upbringing</b>. Heritage is birthright. Usually, it refers to the sapient species to which the character belongs and is evident in her physical traits. Beast-born have animal-like facial features, for example, and pixies have vestigial wings. Upbringing is culture. It reflects early education and experience \u2014 the games they played as children, the skills their caregivers considered the most basic and most important, and what their relatives and neighbors valued or reviled. Heritage and Upbringing are an ongoing conversation carried on from generation to generation. They bleed into one another, such that one can easily be mistaken for the other. A human raised among gray fractals looks human, but he may learn to perceive ghosts the way many members of his adoptive family do, or perhaps he has taken on (or uses makeup to mimic) some of their natural pallor. Nor are Heritages and Upbringings monolithic. Some shifters are deft at adopting disguises, for example, while others manifest tools of wood and metal from their living flesh. Similarly, the children of parents of different Origins could exhibit the traits of one, the other, neither, or both.<br /> <br /> <b>Training:</b> Heroes manifest their abilities in ways as diverse as any of the Clades, and traits normally associated with Clade can often be entangled with those of Heritage and Upbringing. In place of Clade, heroes have Training, which is a combination of <b>Profession</b> and <b>Vocation</b>. A character's Profession is her mundane education, training, and experience \u2014 one that prepared her to practice a trade that would have allowed her to live a quiet, productive life, even if it was as a drunken gambler. Her Vocation is the specialized training and unique experiences that make her well-suited to the life of a hero. For some, Vocation is a natural progression from Profession. The acolyte at the temple receives a mission from the lips of the goddess she serves \u2014 and the supernatural abilities she will need to carry it out. For others, Vocation is a stark departure from their intended Profession. A farmer whose village is burned by raiders might take up arms and become a warrior to protect others like it from future attacks. A hero carries both sets of experiences with him and makes use of everything he has learned. Nor is a character locked into a Vocation once he chooses one. Some heroes are dilettantes in many disciplines (and that dabbling defines them as much as the single-mindedness with which other heroes pursue their Vocations), while others make the decision to surrender one set of supernatural advantages in favor of another.<br /> <br /> <b>Ideals:</b> All heroes live according to a personal code. This has five aspects \u2014 Loyalty, Honor, Service, Conviction, and Adversity. Each character has one Ideal of each type. Importantly, the cohort shares at least one Ideal (other than Loyalty):<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Loyalty:</b> The bonds between members of a cohort are forged in blood and trust, and they are not broken readily or without consequences. Heroes protect one another and stand with one another. This is especially true of members of their cohort, but it extends to other heroes who request (or quite obviously need) aid. A hero doesn't leave her companions to die and doesn't betray them to an enemy.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Honor:</b> Heroes have principles by which they live. They must abide by a set of precepts, whether they are prescriptions (actions they must take) or proscriptions (actions they're prohibited from taking). Those who adhere to this sacred code stand as exemplars of heroic ideals. Those who stray from it stain the reputations of all heroes and risk a swift slide into tragedy.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Service:</b> Most people can't do the things heroes make look so easy. A hero is obligated to protect the innocent and help the needy as best she can. This doesn't demand constant self-sacrifice and unfailing selflessness, but it requires that the hero seldom refuse reasonable requests and that she not discount out of hand those that serve a clear common good while being within her skillset.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Conviction:</b> All heroes are fierce opponents of <i>something</i>, although exactly what depends on the hero. It could be something tangible like dragons or vampires. It could be a pattern of behavior, such as theft or murder. It could be something abstract, such as corruption or poverty. When faced with someone or something that embodies his Conviction, a hero either rises to the occasion or regrets letting the opportunity pass.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Adversity:</b> Heroes aren't perfect. They have limits and make mistakes, but these don't diminish their claim to the title of &quot;hero&quot;. Indeed, repeatedly facing, and rising above, their fears and flaws is a recurring theme in the lives of all heroes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-23T04:06:48-08:00","id":1556934,"title":"","content":"I clearly missed a trick by not scheduling this one two days later, just so I could make the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t039p6xqutU\" target=\"_blank\">Christmas at Ground Zero</a> joke. Ah well. Today we're talking about the Post-Apocalypse genre. I love the elegance of the Resources Merit, but this is one of those genres where it sucks the tension out of a story, so I quickly realized I would need to provide systems for modeling the scarcity that is a feature of the genre, which is what I'm sharing today. As it turns out, some of these systems made a good balancing device for genre-hopping chronicles involving time travelers, visitors to otherworlds, and more, so they wound up in the chapter's introduction.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Resources</span><br /> <br /> Otherworldly vendors seldom accept currencies from worlds not their own. The almighty dollar avails a visitor nothing in the Underworld, and you're not going to be able to pay for dinner on a distant planet with your American Express card. Barter is the norm, which creates three obstacles:<br /> <br /> First, commodities are much less portable than cash or credit cards. Even jewelry is heavy, and it's hard to make change for a diamond ring. Characters can only carry so much before the weight of their trade goods starts to slow them down.<br /> <br /> Second, travelers to otherworlds usually don't have a secure place to store their valuables. Anyone can be robbed and lose everything of value they're carrying at the time, but for a visitor, this is much more likely to be everything they have until they can return home to resupply.<br /> <br /> Third, barter is inefficient. It's rare that a visitor will receive the full value of what she is offering in trade. Less technologically advanced societies usually don't have much to offer in exchange, and more technologically advanced societies usually won't consider the visitor's goods worth trading.<br /> <br /> <b>Systems</b><br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Resources:</b> Characters with the Resources Merit can't call upon it when in an otherworld.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Capacity:</b> Characters can reasonably carry or wear a number of items of Size 2 no greater than their Strength + Stamina or their Size (whichever is greater) without difficulty. A character who exceeds this suffers the Fatigued Condition after six hours of carrying the burden. A character can't carry more than twice as many Size 2 items as their Strength + Stamina (or twice their Size, if greater). Three Size 1 items count as one Size 2 item. Items of Size 3 or more (but which are still portable) count as two Size 2 items. A vehicle can carry its Size in Size 2 items, in addition to its driver (and any equipment she is carrying); passengers (with carried equipment) subtract their Size from this capacity.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Barter:</b> An item can be bartered for one of <i>lesser</i> Availability with a successful Social action (a Presence or Manipulation + Persuasion roll resisted by Resolve is the most common). Acquiring something of equal Availability generally requires an extended negotiation that can be modeled with the Social Maneuvering system.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Advanced Technology</span><br /> <br /> Time travelers and aliens from more technologically advanced worlds often bring some of their tools with them. Whether it's a shotgun in the Dark Ages or walkie talkies on a distant planet, these high-tech devices can give a visitor a considerable advantage. However, the production and maintenance of advanced technology relies upon the availability of other advanced technology. A gun becomes useless in the absence of ammunition, and creating ammunition for a modern firearm requires the availability of materials and tools suitable for the job, and those tools and materials often rely on the availability of <i>other</i> tools and materials.<br /> <br /> Advanced technology deteriorates quickly without regular maintenance, and this maintenance is usually only available in places where that technology is readily available.<br /> <br /> <b>System:</b> At the end of any chapter in which a piece of advanced technology is used, roll a dice pool equal to 6 \u2013 Availability. On a failed roll, the device's owner takes a Beat, and the device ceases to function or breaks until it can be repaired using tools and materials at least as advanced as those used in its creation.<br /> <br /> <b>Durable:</b> Some advanced devices are built to keep working even in the absence of adequate maintenance and materials. Increase the device's Availability by one to increase the dice pool above by two \u2014 or increase it by two to increase the dice pool by four. So, an assault rifle (Availability 3) normally rolls 3 dice (6 \u2013 3 = 3). A durable one becomes Availability 4 but rolls 4 dice (6 \u2013 4 + 2 = 4) or becomes Availability 5 and rolls 5 dice (6 \u2013 5 + 4 = 5).<br /> <br /> <b>Prototypes:</b> Prototypes purchased with Resources or otherwise acquired without spending Experiences are subject to the rules for advanced technology; eventually, they will break, run out of ammo/battery, or otherwise cease to function as intended. Prototypes acquired through the Prototype Merit aren't subject to this deterioration.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-25T04:00:09-08:00","id":1556982,"title":"","content":"I'm fighting the urge to make a Life Day joke so hard right now. The Space Opera genre was a lot of fun to write, perhaps because I've spent my whole life consuming media in that vein. It provides all the excuse anyone could ever want to include mecha and starships. Today's excerpt is the introduction to the technology that makes this whole setting go, which relies on the souls of Remade partially excised from their bodies or locked in the agony of perpetually maintained End Stage. It touches upon Exos (this setting's mechas), which you can read about in greater detail for free at the Patreon (and patrons can access the full text).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Soultech</span><br /> <br /> Astronomical distances coupled with hard limits on the speed of travel between points trillions or quadrillions of miles apart were enough to make even the most starry-eyed space enthusiast despair. But that was before the genesis of soultech. For centuries, fringe scientists studied the phenomenon of Divergence, which seemingly unleashed more energy from the human body than anything of comparable mass could contain \u2014 short of a macroscopic matter-antimatter annihilation. While the precise understanding of why the human soul can yield such tremendous energy when it is cracked remains elusive, designing technology capable of harnessing it ultimately proved within reach. The resulting field, known as soultech, allowed humanity to catapult itself into an interstellar civilization. This has placed Deviants at the center of all human empires \u2014 sometimes as rulers but more often as soldiers on battleships, in starfighters, or as part of invasion forces. &quot;Soultech&quot; is often used in the generic sense of any technology that only functions when operated by Remade.<br /> <br /> <b>Soul Gear:</b> This includes soul weapons (soul guns, soul swords, etc.), soul armor, and any number of other pieces of soultech personal equipment. They are Prototypes (<b>Clades Companion</b>, p. 56) that can only be used by Broken.<br /> <br /> <b>Break Drive:</b> Interstellar spacecraft are equipped with break drives, which harness the energy of Remade crewmembers to achieve &quot;breaks&quot; \u2014 instantaneous jumps across the vast distances between star systems. With a few rare (and still highly experimental) exceptions, humans are the only species capable of powering a break drive.<br /> <br /> <b>Soul Ship:</b> Also known as &quot;break ships&quot;, spacecraft with break drives are veritable sitting ducks when they have no Remade crew aboard. Their hulls are no less durable (nor are they any less massive), and they're still capable of performing crude orbital maneuvers and of maintaining life support, but none of their soultech systems function, including shields, thrusters, soul cannons, and sweeps.<br /> <br /> <b>Exos:</b> Exos (sing. Exo) is short for &quot;exoskeletons&quot;. Exos are a form of power armor that ranges in size from merely as large as a wheeled ground vehicle to the size of a small skyscraper. They require a single Deviant pilot, and they can be as heavily armed and armored as a small soultech spacecraft. The majority are humanoid, deployed by empires as ground assault vehicles that emulate human motion and magnify the Variations of their pilots. Less commonly, they resemble terrestrial vehicles such as tanks, seagoing vessels, or aircraft. Although some Exos can fly, or even achieve orbital velocity, they lack break drives and so aren't suitable for travel over interstellar distances. Like soul gear and soul ships, Exos require calibration.<br /> <br /> <b>Calibration:</b> All soultech (soul gear, soul ship systems, and Exos) requires calibration before it will function. Calibrating Exos and soul ship systems (other than break drives) excises a Variation from a Deviant and installs it in the soultech. This permanently burns out the capability from the Deviant except when he is using soultech. Calibrating a soul cannon based on a Cutting Firearms Lash causes the Remade to permanently lose access to his Lash, for example, but he can still use any soul cannon, Exo, or other soul gear. Whenever a soul ship is constructed, it's a common (but by no means universal) practice to have the Remade who calibrated it become permanent members of its crew.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Breaking</span><br /> <br /> A break drive can transport a ship to any point in space whose relative coordinates are known to its navigator. This comes with a few caveats: First, because light can take centuries to reach an observer across interstellar distances, a star or planet's location is never precisely where it appears to be. Second, objects in space are in constant motion, which makes it easier to make a break for a point near a star system than it is to break for a planet orbiting a given star. Third, unless the traveler knows both her origin point and destination well, she must first determine the direction and relative distance, which involves parallax navigation. Finally, not all space hazards are visible at a great distance.<br /> <br /> Practically speaking, this means that breaking directly from the orbit of one planet to the orbit of another several light years away is impossible. Instead, a soul ship must make several consecutive breaks with a short stop at each interim point to take readings and plot the next break. While one approach is to make breaks of decreasing distances, gradually &quot;zooming in&quot; on one's destination, this is seldom done except by those exploring uncharted space. It's far more common to break to one of a few thousand guide stars, which have minimal hazards, well-charted planets, and, importantly, nearby settlements that can render aid in the event of an emergency. A break drive failure a few light minutes from a space station is inconvenient and expensive; one several light years from the nearest inhabited planet could mean lifelong exile or worse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-25T21:58:05-08:00","id":1557002,"title":"","content":"Oh please tell me the Genre section has a Superheroes section!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When the cat's a Stray, the mice will pray...</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25851-thestray7","name":"TheStray7","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25851_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"176"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-26T02:59:54-08:00","id":1557006,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheStray7</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1555429-black-vans-a-storyteller-s-guide-to-deviant-the-renegades?p=1557002#post1557002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh please tell me the Genre section has a Superheroes section!</div> </div> </div> </div>It does! I'll be previewing it on Saturday.<br /> <br /> I'll warn that it doesn't do every kind of superhero story. Four-color comics aren't really it's focus so much as stories like X-Men, Heroes, or The Boys.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-26T03:41:21-08:00","id":1557007,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eric Zawadzki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1555429-black-vans-a-storyteller-s-guide-to-deviant-the-renegades?p=1557006#post1557006\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It does! I'll be previewing it on Saturday.<br /> <br /> I'll warn that it doesn't do every kind of superhero story. Four-color comics aren't really it's focus so much as stories like X-Men, Heroes, or The Boys.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fine with me, that's the sort of superhero story I've been thinking about running with Deviant anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When the cat's a Stray, the mice will pray...</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25851-thestray7","name":"TheStray7","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25851_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"176"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-27T04:00:19-08:00","id":1557041,"title":"","content":"From the moment I mentioned genre-swapping Deviant, we all knew I was going to arrive at superheroes, and here we are. The genre is enormous, and settling on exactly what flavor of superheroes I wanted to focus on was no mean feat. In some ways, I found I couldn't decide and ended up making it seven different settings depending on the current public sentiment towards the Remade. Today I'm going to show off the Sentiment mechanics.<br /> <br /> I've also posted the genre's modified and new Merits as a free excerpt at the Patreon (and patrons can view the full text).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Sentiment</span><br /> <br /> Sentiment is a measure of the public's collective opinion of Remade on a continuum from Glorified (the most enthusiastic) to At War (the most hostile). A chronicle's Sentiment will shape the cohort's interactions with the public and public institutions. The Storyteller should determine Sentiment in consultation with the players, as this will color all superhumans' interactions with the public and public institutions.<br /> <br /> High and low Sentiment grant mechanical benefits \u2014 whether only to Devoted/Renegades or to all Diverged. Sentiment also determines what kinds of behavior move it more positively or negatively.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Glorified</span><br /> <br /> Baselines expect Remade to solve all their problems and to spare them every hardship and inconvenience. This sentiment is most appropriate in a setting that explores the stultifying experience of being a public resource who faces constant public scrutiny and unrealistic expectations.<br /> <br /> <b>Improving Sentiment:</b> A superhuman saves or restores many lives or preserves something of similarly great significance to many people (such as a landmark or priceless work of art).<br /> <br /> <b>Worsening Sentiment:</b> A significant harm occurs when the Diverged conceivably could have been present in the scene to prevent it.<br /> <br /> <b>Remade:</b> In addition to the benefits of Positive (see below), at the end of each chapter, the cohort generates Effort that they may invest in conspiracy Projects. The amount of Effort is equal to half current Standing (rounded up).<br /> <br /> <b>Devoted:</b> In addition to the benefits of Positive, once per chapter, the Devoted may negate the events of a single scene for the purpose of worsening Sentiment.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Positive</span><br /> <br /> Baselines expect superhumans to use their supernatural talents to further the interests of humanity (although exactly what that means varies from one Baseline to the next). Positive Sentiment is most appropriate in a setting where Remade are superheroes with the full support of a number of Baselines, whether they're government authorities, members of a spiritual community, or a conspiracy.<br /> <br /> <b>Improving Sentiment:</b> The Diverged save a life, prevent destruction of property (including public property) or natural beauty, or apprehend a Renegade. Alternatively, one or more of them risks harm or sacrifices something significant in the service of the established order and public good.<br /> <br /> <b>Worsening Sentiment:</b> A superhuman is visibly present in the scene and doesn't intervene to prevent a significant harm or to accomplish a good deed (even a small one). Alternatively, harm occurs while no Remade are present, and none publicly acknowledge it or help with recovery.<br /> <br /> <b>Remade:</b> All Diverged benefit from the Qualified Immunity Merit (p. XX).<br /> <br /> <b>Devoted:</b> In addition to the benefits of Favorable (see below), the Devoted may take a conspiracy action once per chapter, treating conspiracy Attributes as equal to Standing + 2. If unused, this action is lost.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Favorable</span><br /> <br /> The public regards the Diverged as interesting and exciting, like a new technology with a lot of untapped potential. Favorable Sentiment is most appropriate for a setting in which superhumans often play at being superheroes but haven't gained widespread support from Baseline institutions.<br /> <br /> <b>Improving Sentiment:</b> Diverged use their Variations in the service of others or the public good without the expectation of reward. Alternatively, they accept compensation or donations (in advance or after the fact) to save a life, to prevent destruction of property (including public property) or natural beauty, or to apprehend a Renegade.<br /> <br /> <b>Worsening Sentiment:</b> The Remade publicly try but fail to prevent a harm or accomplish a good deed (whether compensated or not).<br /> <br /> <b>Devoted:</b> These superhumans wield influence within Baseline society by making friends and proving that Remade can be valuable to their communities. The Devoted receives two dots of Allies, Anonymity, Contacts, Mentor, Reputation, Resources, Retainer, Safe Place, Staff, or Status (all are in <b>Deviant</b> except for Staff, which appears in <b>Devoted Companion</b> \u2014 p. 18), which can be shared by other Devoted in their cohort.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Neutral</span><br /> <br /> Either Diverged are such a new phenomenon that Baselines haven't yet formed a collective opinion of them, or they're polarizing figures with a close to even mix of strong proponents and detractors. Neutral Sentiment is most appropriate for a setting in which Divergence is a new phenomenon (or one only recently entering the public consciousness).<br /> <br /> Neither Devoted nor Renegades enjoy any special benefits due to Sentiment.<br /> <br /> <b>Improving Sentiment:</b> A superhuman uses his Variations in a context that shows them to be useful and beneficial (even if he's paid to do so).<br /> <br /> <b>Worsening Sentiment:</b> A Remade uses his Variations in a context that shows their limitations, the danger they represent, or the thorny moral and ethical issues they raise.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Unfriendly</span><br /> <br /> The Diverged have captured public imagination, and Baselines everywhere shake their heads as they recount the latest news stories about people with dangerous powers engaged in reckless or criminal behavior. Unfriendly Sentiment is most appropriate in a setting where superhumans are mistrusted or face prejudice.<br /> <br /> <b>Improving Sentiment:</b> A superhuman publicly embraces the principle of serving Baselines and/or Baseline institutions, or encourages other Remade to do so.<br /> <br /> <b>Worsening Sentiment:</b> One of the Diverged uses her Variations to commit a nonviolent crime, or her Variation use causes minor property damage (such as flattening a car or destroying a city sidewalk) or serious injury to a Baseline.<br /> <br /> <b>Renegade:</b> Superhumans use their abilities to accumulate resources and attract those who crave proximity to power or wield supernatural influence of their own. The Renegade receives two dots of Allies, Anonymity, Contacts, Mentor, Reputation, Resources, Retainer, Safe Place, Staff, or Status (all are in <b>Deviant</b> except for Staff, which appears in <b>Devoted Companion </b>\u2014 p. 18), which can be shared with other Renegades in her cohort.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Hostile</span><br /> <br /> The public blames the Remade for much of what goes wrong in the world, and superhumans with known identities live under the constant threat of harassment if not outright mob violence. Hostile Sentiment is most appropriate in a setting where Diverged face violence if they are recognized for what they are, incentivizing secrecy and subtlety.<br /> <br /> <b>Improving Sentiment:</b> A superhuman doesn't use his Variations when it's obvious to witnesses it would have benefited him to do so.<br /> <br /> <b>Worsening Sentiment:</b> Diverged use their Variations to commit a violent crime, to commit a serious nonviolent crime (such as a bank, casino, or jewelry heist), or to cause moderate property damage (such as burning down a house or causing several city blocks to lose power). Alternatively, their Variations kill a Baseline or hurt many Baselines.<br /> <br /> <b>Remade:</b> All Remade enjoy the benefits of the Armed and Extremely Dangerous Merit (<b>Deviant</b>, p. 100), which applies against all Baselines not expecting to encounter superhumans.<br /> <br /> <b>Renegade:</b> In addition to the benefits of Unfriendly (see above), Renegades count their Scar Power, Scar Finesse, and Scar Resistance as one higher (to a maximum of 6).<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">At War</span><br /> <br /> Baseline society regards Diverged as an existential threat to humanity and is actively attempting to eliminate them \u2014 whether by &quot;curing&quot; or exterminating them. They might still use Devoted to further this goal, but loyal superhumans are merely lower priority targets. At War Sentiment is most appropriate in settings where Remade are openly hunted and killed as monsters by Baseline governments and vigilantes alike.<br /> <br /> <b>Improving Sentiment:</b> One of the Diverged is nice to a Baseline without expectation of a reward, even if her kindness costs her nothing.<br /> <br /> <b>Worsening Sentiment:</b> A superhuman uses her Variations to cause major property damage (such as destroying a landmark or blowing up a skyscraper) or widespread injury and death to Baselines.<br /> <br /> <b>Remade:</b> In addition to the benefits of Hostile (see above), all Diverged quickly learn to use their powers creatively in order to survive, and may improvise a Technique once per <i>scene</i> instead of once per chapter (<b>Shallow Graves</b>, p. 199).<br /> <br /> <b>Renegade:</b> In addition to the benefits of Hostile (see above), once per chapter, a Renegade may gain access to two dots of the Manticore Companion and/or Prototype Merits, which may be shared with other Renegades in her cohort to gain access to a more powerful Manticore/Prototype.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Moving Sentiment</span><br /> <br /> The previous section provides examples of actions that increase (improve) or decrease (worsen) Sentiment. The more positive the current Sentiment, the more kinds of actions worsen Sentiment, and the more negative current Sentiment, the more kinds of actions improve Sentiment.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Actions that <i>improve</i> Sentiment at <i>higher</i> tiers of Sentiment also improve Sentiment at lower tiers. Saving the lives of dozens of people (which improves Sentiment at Glorified Sentiment) still improves Sentiment if Sentiment is Neutral or At War, for example.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Actions that <i>worsen</i> Sentiment at <i>lower</i> tiers of Sentiment also worsen it at higher tiers. Destroying a cherished landmark (which worsens Sentiment at At War Sentiment) still worsens Sentiment if Sentiment is Neutral or Glorified, for example.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Actions that <i>improve</i> Sentiment at <i>lower</i> tiers of Sentiment don't improve it at higher tiers. Giving sweets to children has no measurable impact on the public perception of superhumans when society expects Remade to save lives without compensation.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Actions that <i>worsen</i> Sentiment at <i>higher</i> tiers of Sentiment don't worsen it at lower tiers. Failing to halt a runaway train on the other side of the city (which worsens Sentiment at Glorified Sentiment) isn't going to be much of a public relations hit in a world where Diverged are the leading cause of train derailments (current Sentiment is At War).<br /> <br /> Actions only affect Sentiment if they're witnessed by Baselines who don't personally know the superhumans involved.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Changing Sentiment</span><br /> <br /> Through concerted effort, a cohort can change public perception of all Remade. While in principle <i>any</i> superhuman can shift Sentiment, maintain focus on the actions of the players' characters (and those of their close allies and major antagonists) when determining the direction Sentiment shifts.<br /> <br /> \u2022 At the end of each story, Sentiment can stay the same, or it can improve or worsen by one step, depending on the events of the story. If Remade mostly took actions that could improve Sentiment, it will improve; if they mostly took actions that could worsen Sentiment, it worsens.<br /> <br /> \u2022 At the Storyteller's discretion, Sentiment may improve or worsen by one step at the end of a chapter that had an especially noteworthy event in favor of moving Sentiment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-27T07:32:06-08:00","id":1557043,"title":"","content":"Neat to see that the Renegade benefit for being At War has gotten a more concrete benefit, and the new and modified Merits on the Patreon are definitely interesting. Am I correct in reading the &quot;as an optional effect that costs two additional Merits dots&quot; effects on Beloved Community, Sacred Code, and Victim Profile as mutually exclusive with both each other (within each Merit) and their prerequisite branches due to the Merit(s) not going to eight dots?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-27T12:04:44-08:00","id":1557050,"title":"","content":"Correct. Not visible in the free previews, but each allegiance get two free dots of one of the three Merits, so to get 8 dots of any given one you need to spend 6 XP/Merit dots and belong to the right team. Here's what those look like:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Allegiance</span><br /> <br /> Each superhuman holds one of six Allegiances: Advocate, Champion, Enforcer, Rebel, Reformer or Rogue. Allegiance, along with Origin, determines the Remade's starting mix of Loyalty and Conviction Touchstones. Half are considered Devoted Allegiances for the purposes of determining how furthering Touchstones replenishes Willpower and heals Instability, while the other half are Renegade Allegiances.<br /> <br /> <b>Advocate (Devoted):</b> These superhumans subscribe to a system of beliefs that drives them to build, create, or preserve something of value. Most are religious, or at least abide by a principled code of ethics, and they tend towards altruism (and philanthropy, if they have the means). They begin play with 3 Loyalty Touchstones, no Conviction Touchstones, and two dots of the Sacred Code Merit (p. XX), which counts as a fourth Loyalty Touchstone.<br /> <br /> <i>Rhetoric: For the purposes of Social Maneuvering, the Advocate counts the impression level of hostile characters as poor. She instead enjoys a +2 bonus on Social actions other than Intimidation that target hostile characters.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Champion (Devoted):</b> These Remade embody Loyalty and service to their communities. They are the most likely of the Allegiances to behave as traditional superheroes and take any injury suffered by those under their protection personally. They begin play with 2 Loyalty Touchstones, 1 Conviction Touchstone, and two dots of the Beloved Community Merit (p. XX), which counts as a third Loyalty Touchstone.<br /> <br /> <i>Figurehead: Whenever the Champion takes an action that would improve Sentiment, she gains the Inspired Condition to take an action that would improve Sentiment.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Enforcer (Devoted):</b> These Diverged serve an organization that sanctions them to perform violence in its name. They might be law enforcement, military, or the Devoted servants of conspiracies. They begin play with 2 Loyalty Touchstones, 1 Conviction Touchstone, and two dots of the Victim Profile Merit (p. XX), which counts as a second Conviction Touchstone.<br /> <br /> <i>Just Desserts: Whenever the Enforcer destroys a target meeting his Victim Profile, his actions during the scene never worsen Sentiment and he gains the Inspired Condition for an action against another that matches his Victim Profile.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Rebel (Renegade):</b> These superhumans regard themselves as protectors of a group of their choosing and do their best to eliminate anyone who threatens their chosen family. The best of them are vigilantes and folk heroes beloved by those they protect. The worst are terrorists. They begin play with 1 Loyalty Touchstone, 2 Conviction Touchstones, and two dots of the Beloved Community Merit (p. XX), which counts as a second Loyalty Touchstone.<br /> <br /> <i>Friends Everywhere: Once per chapter, the Rebel's player may announce that a member of the Remade's Beloved Community is present or promptly arrives to render assistance. This is usually a Baseline (or a small group of them) but is always someone in a position to help the Broken in a small but notable way.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Reformer (Renegade): </b>These Diverged adhere to a system of beliefs that drives them to tear down existing systems and those who defend them. Although not all of them resort to force, they are unquestionably more interested in removing enemies than in winning over friends. They begin play with no Loyalty Touchstones, 3 Conviction Touchstones, and two dots of the Sacred Code Merit (p. XX), which counts as a Loyalty Touchstone.<br /> <br /> <i>Dossier: Once per chapter, the Reformer may gain the Informed Condition regarding one of her Conviction Touchstones.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Rogue (Renegade):</b> These superhumans use their powers to carry out their fantasies, most of which involve hurting people. While some have legitimate grievances against their targets \u2014 such as to avenge themselves against those who murdered people close to them \u2014 many are bullies, if not serial killers. They begin play with no Loyalty Touchstones, 3 Conviction Touchstones, and two dots of the Victim Profile Merit (p. XX), which counts as a fourth Conviction Loyalty Touchstone.<br /> <br /> <i>Bogeyman:</i> Whenever the Rogue takes an action that would worsen Sentiment, she gains the Inspired Condition to take an action that would worsen Sentiment.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Origin</span><br /> <br /> A superhuman's Origin grants one Conviction or Loyalty Touchstone as described in Deviant.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Changing Allegiance</span><br /> <br /> Remade may change Allegiance once per story.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Changing from one Devoted Allegiance to another (or from one Renegade Allegiance to another) has no special requirements.<br /> \u2022 Changing from a Devoted Allegiance to a Renegade Allegiance requires that the character has at least as many Conviction Touchstones (including the Victim Profile Merit granted by her Allegiance) as Loyalty Touchstones.<br /> \u2022 Changing from a Renegade Allegiance to a Devoted Allegiance requires that the character has more Loyalty Touchstones (including the Beloved Community or Sacred Code Merit granted by her Allegiance) than Conviction Touchstones.<br /> <br /> The benefits of the old Allegiance (including its bonus Merit) are immediately lost and the new one's gained.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-12-30T04:00:50-08:00","id":1557139,"title":"","content":"A final section at the end of Chapter Five is meant to pull together everything in Black Vans (and all the other books of the Deviant: The Renegades line) and underline what this book is meant for. It's short, so I'm sharing it all for free \u2013 the first half here and the second half in a separate post on the <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a>.<br /> <br /> I hope you've enjoyed the previews. <b>Black Vans</b> will be on sale at on the Storyteller's Vault on January 1st. I hope you'll pick up a copy once it is, but more than anything, I hope you will find it useful in running your Deviant: The Renegades chronicles!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">One Piece at a Time</span><br /> <br /> A <b>Deviant: The Renegades</b> chronicle isn't built on an assembly line. Every troupe has a different idea for what kind of chronicle they want to play \u2014 if not which one they want to play <i>this </i>time. The game's core book offered a huge variety of potential characters and stories, and four supplements of new character options, alternate setting assumptions, and system hacks have only multiplied the number of possibilities. You can't do <i>everything</i> in <b>Deviant</b>, but you can do a <i>lot</i> if you put your mind to it.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">DIY Ethos</span><br /> <br /> Let's talk about what goes into designing an alternate Deviant setting.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Some Assembly Required</span><br /> <br /> Before you start tinkering with game systems, it's important to know what kind of stories you want to tell in your chronicle.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Place</span><br /> <br /> Consider the where of your chronicle.<br /> <br /> <b>Genre:</b> The genre or sub-genre is a good starting point. Modern horror is the default, but we've presented six other options, any of which could be smudged, mixed, or modified \u2014 and a motivated Storyteller could even build a new one from scratch.<br /> <br /> <b>Theme and Mood:</b> A well-built chronicle explores central themes and conveys a certain mood. This doesn't mean every chapter rigidly adheres to these guide stars, but events should return to them again and again. Traditionally, <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> games have called out two (theme and mood), while the genre hacks in this chapter each outline four, but there isn't a magical number of these as long as the list is short.<br /> <br /> <b>Backdrop:</b> The physical setting flows from its genre, themes, and mood. Pick qualities and set pieces that reinforce the overall flavor you're going for. Some of these may suggest mechanics, as well, but the focus here is on creating an interesting place for events to take place in \u2014 whether it's a single neighborhood at a defined point in time or an entire network of interdimensional outposts scattered across multiple parallel timelines.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">People</span><br /> <br /> Think about the who of your chronicle. This includes Storyteller characters but should focus on the players' characters.<br /> <br /> <b>Commonalities:</b> What's the common ground that draws (and keeps) the members of the cohort together? This can be circumstantial, such as being made by the same conspiracy or marooned on the same planet, but it could be a shared interest, such as studying ghosts or being a team of athletes.<br /> <br /> <b>Differences:</b> How do the members of the cohort distinguish themselves from their fellows? This is often as simple as having different Variations or choosing different Skills, but it might not be in a chronicle where everyone was chosen for Divergence because they shared certain skills and were given largely identical Variations. Players like their characters to have some niche, both in terms of capabilities and personality.<br /> <br /> <b>Main Event:</b> What is the central gameplay loop of the chronicle (i.e., how the characters are expected to spend most of their time)? This could easily be two to four related activities; in core <b>Deviant</b>, it's &quot;elude/fight the conspiracy and further Conviction/Loyalty Touchstones&quot;.<br /> <br /> <b>Sideshows:</b> How much flexibility do the characters have to pursue activities that are outside of the main event, and what kinds of activities are most common? A chronicle in which a conspiracy pursues the cohort constantly is different from one where a conspiracy must share the spotlight with a parade of monsters of the week.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Motivations</span><br /> <br /> Focus on the whys of the chronicle \u2014 specifically, why the cohort does the things they're meant to do. These motivations have three axes:<br /> <br /> <b>Narrative/Mechanical:</b> Narrative motivations invoke the chronicle's story and play on the players' desire for an engaging plot and interesting characters. Mechanical motivations offer rewards for some behaviors and threaten to punish others. Which axis works best at your table depends heavily on who sits at it. While some players embrace genre conventions and swallow plot hooks without question, most benefit from occasional mechanical nudges to keep them on-task. And for some, mechanical reinforcement is a primary driver of in-character behavior.<br /> <br /> <b>External/Internal:</b> Some motivations exert pressure on the characters from within, while others do so from without. The conspiracy's actions, Projects, and most of its key agents are external motivations. Aspirations and Scars are usually internal motivations. Touchstones and Instability have qualities of both internal and external motivations. Include a mix of motivations. External motivations are unifying, usually drawing in the whole cohort to address a threat or seize an opportunity, but they can feel a bit impersonal. Internal motivations, meanwhile, are extremely relevant to the players whose characters pursue them but are less likely to motivate the whole cohort.<br /> <br /> <b>Carrots/Sticks:</b> Most methods of motivation boil down to either rewarding or punishing player behavior. In <b>Deviant</b>, these carrots and sticks are written into the mechanics. Characters receive carrots for playing up their Scars and furthering their Touchstones. They're met with sticks when they Falter, choose not to engage with their Touchstones, or ignore the conspiracy (which unleashes nastier Projects). Both approaches work, although sticks can generate player resentment if used too liberally.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Taking Stock</span><br /> <br /> Once you know the where, who, and why of your chronicle, make some decisions about how to render them mechanically. This can sound intimidating, but <b>Deviant</b> offers many systems and options that can be adapted to represent other things. You see this throughout <b>Black Vans</b> \u2014 taking systems from the core, <b>The Clades Companion</b>, <b>The Devoted Companion</b>, and <b>Shallow Graves</b> and redefining or adding to them so that they represent a non-Deviant character or convey a theme or mood appropriate to a new genre. Here are some of the tools already at your disposal:<br /> <br /> <b>Adaptations:</b> Adaptations help define different &quot;types&quot; (Clades) of Deviant characters. Making all of them Universal removes much of that distinction, while mandating new arrays of Adaptations multiplies the number of Clades. Disallowing reset Adaptations greatly reduces the use of limited-use abilities and should be done with caution.<br /> <br /> <b>Forms:</b> Core Forms represent a shift in capabilities (rather than a net increase or decrease in power), while Advanced Forms allow the investment of Merit dots (and potentially Experiences) to make them a hybrid of Variations, Scars, and Merits all in one customizable package. Despite their flavor, they can be reskinned to reflect other situations that match their mechanics. Symbiote, for example, could represent a high fantasy priest's powers being granted to him by a goddess who may weaken or deny them outright if his use of them isn't in line with her goals. Situational Forms like Allayed and Visitor often reflect adverse conditions and methods of compensating for them. Greater Forms and Lesser Forms (and Scar Forms) are essentially Forms nested within other Forms, and although we use them as the basis for crossover, the concept could be used to build any number of remarkable new creatures or specialized concepts.<br /> <br /> <b>Merits:</b> Most Core Merits give the character options not available to others, while the rest enhance existing capabilities in some notable way. Deviant-Only Merits interact with Remade systems, often increasing the utility of Variations in ways beyond the scope of Techniques. Most narrowly defined, nifty capabilities can be rendered as Merits. Variation-Specific Merits expand the scope of a Variation beyond the normal 5-dot range and/or create new capabilities that reflect the synergy between two Variations.<br /> <br /> <b>Variations/Scars/Standard Deviations:</b> In the core, these represent the powers (and their costs) of Broken. Although often designed as packages of related abilities, Variations are eminently customizable using the inspired Variation process in <b>Shallow Graves</b>. Natural Variations and Scars (and Forms that increase/decrease Size) shift the conversation from &quot;what you can do&quot; to &quot;what you naturally are&quot;, allowing the introduction of extraterrestrial species and extradimensional visitors alike. Storytellers shouldn't hesitate to forbid some of these if they're wrong for the intended chronicle, or to mandate those required to make a chronicle work.<br /> <br /> <b>Instability:</b> Instability is at once a premium fuel trait and a looming existential threat. At base, it provides a mechanical carrot/stick. It reinforces a genre's tropes, which is why this chapter so often leans on it to do exactly that.<br /> <br /> <b>Conspiracies:</b> Although cohorts face individual enemies, <b>Deviant</b> has extensive systems for collective antagonists \u2014 especially with the expanded conspiracy rules presented in <b>The Devoted Companion</b>. As we've already illustrated, they need not represent literal conspiracies. They can reflect a hostile environment, collective frenemies, or even the pure narrative imperative of a Storyteller to introduce new complications to a story (especially if the cohort isn't being proactive about confronting their source).<br /> <br /> <b>Rewards: </b>While healing Instability is one way to reward desired behavior, Willpower and Beats are the main reward mechanisms. Willpower is a quick, temporary hit. Beats, on the other hand, have minimal impact right now but represent the long-term growth of a character. Conspiracy Beats in <b>The Devoted Companion</b> and Static in <b>Shallow Graves</b> offer ways to reward behavior without relying on Willpower, Instability healing, or Beats, but including too many reward mechanics can make character advancement unnecessarily complicated.<br /> <br /> <b>Deviant Equipment:</b> Although implied in the core and rendered for antagonists in <b>The Devoted Companion</b>, <b>The Clades Companion</b> presents details about equipment that interacts with Variations and Scars directly (Repressors/Boosters/Neutralizers) or emulates them (Prototypes). A character heavily invested in Prototypes might be a gadgeteer, especially if they exchange most of their starting Variation Magnitude for extra Merit dots (p. XX) or have the Inventor Merit (p. XX).<br /> <br /> <b>Ephemeral Entities:</b> These beings are naturally out of phase with the physical world. Although <b>The Clades Companion</b> presents them as antagonists, they could be adapted as player characters, albeit not without some caveats. First, the active conspiracy (or other collective foe) must have some means of pursuing and threatening them. Second, if they're capable of traveling to an otherworld, the rest of the cohort must also have some way of doing so.<br /> <br /> <b>Techniques:</b> As presented in <b>Shallow Graves</b>, Techniques resemble Variation-Specific Merits. They differ in two ways. First, they can be used (in a limited way) without needing to purchase them. Second, they all have the same cost. Although specific to Variations, the premise could be expanded to include Skills, allowing those with significant mundane training (say, 3 or more dots) to pull off stunts that leave hobbyists gawking but are still roughly equivalent to a 1-dot Merit when Mastered.<br /> <br /> <b>Splats and Genres:</b> The various pieces of Chapters Four and Five of this book are meant to work together to convey a specific setting or include the characters and themes of other games, but they can also be mixed and tweaked to create something new.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-01-01T00:24:44-08:00","id":1557172,"title":"","content":"Black Vans is now live on the <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\">Storytellers Vault</a>.<br /> <br /> For any errata feedback, please fill out <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfz6bWM7omyzr9YGOn6RuxHrhoOavWEj1q9nmgscnLVhOYJ3w/viewform?usp=header\" target=\"_blank\">the errata form here</a> until 1/31/2026.<br /> <br /> <img data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/147099381/4f1ad700427c4d0398a97aa7292538ce/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.png?token-hash=wfd0kyeGgpTF6elplOKC0gASv-OAxq-dteAVnWp3OOs%3D&amp;token-time=1768521600\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/147099381/4f1ad700427c4d0398a97aa7292538ce/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.png?token-hash=wfd0kyeGgpTF6elplOKC0gASv-OAxq-dteAVnWp3OOs%3D&amp;token-time=1768521600\" data-thumb-url=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/147099381/4f1ad700427c4d0398a97aa7292538ce/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.png?token-hash=wfd0kyeGgpTF6elplOKC0gASv-OAxq-dteAVnWp3OOs%3D&amp;token-time=1768521600\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/><br /> <img data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/147099381/2d481422ea184b28afb655c7e55d3aad/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.jpg?token-hash=zQ0VTwFhWWJL9Dqv1hOuvS85aM3MZISy6KoJXejSIBk%3D&amp;token-time=1768521600\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/147099381/2d481422ea184b28afb655c7e55d3aad/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.jpg?token-hash=zQ0VTwFhWWJL9Dqv1hOuvS85aM3MZISy6KoJXejSIBk%3D&amp;token-time=1768521600\" data-thumb-url=\"https://c10.patreonusercontent.com/4/patreon-media/p/post/147099381/2d481422ea184b28afb655c7e55d3aad/eyJhIjoxLCJ3Ijo4MjB9/1.jpg?token-hash=zQ0VTwFhWWJL9Dqv1hOuvS85aM3MZISy6KoJXejSIBk%3D&amp;token-time=1768521600\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/><br /> Broken on the run from the conspiracies quickly learn to distrust many kinds of vehicles \u2014 limousines and sedans with dark-tinted windows, police cruisers, helicopters, and a whole host of transports used by the world's militaries \u2014 but none more fully encapsulate the dread and suspicion of escaped Deviants than a handful of black vans gathered in one place. Too often these have served as a conveyance for masked, heavily armed capture crews, fanatical, wild-eyed cultists, or dangerously obsessed Devoted and their snarling Manticore companions.<br /> <br /> <b>Black Vans</b> is a Storyteller's guide to <b>Deviant: The Renegades</b>. This book contains:<br /> <br /> \u2022 Extensive advice for planning and executing a successful <b>Deviant: The Renegades</b> chronicle, including discussion of common narrative devices, a detailed look at the game's mechanics, and optional and alternate rules to help Storytellers finetune its systems.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Tools for quickly creating Baseline and Devoted antagonists, foils, and allies.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Systems and narrative suggestions for incorporating the other strange inhabitants of the <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> into a <b>Deviant: The Renegades</b> chronicle, complete with guidance for crossover play.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Six new genres for Deviants to inhabit \u2014 Cyberpunk, Dark Eras, High Fantasy, Post-Apocalypse, Space Opera, and Superhero Emergence \u2014 complete with new and modified systems for bringing the chronicles set in them to life.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"http://rpg.drivethrustuff.com/browse.php?x=0&amp;y=0&amp;author=Eric%20Zawadzki\" target=\"_blank\">Onyx Path Freelancer</a></b> and former Deviant line developer.<br /> <br /> Deviant projects now on STV: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/486079/Shallow-Graves?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Shallow Graves</b></a> and <a href=\"https://site.storytellersvault.com/product/550535/Black-Vans?affiliate_id=54122\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Black Vans</b></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://patreon.com/Dayzdark\" target=\"_blank\">Updates and excerpts of current projects available here</a>.\u200b","edited":{"userid":1094,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","user":"Eric Zawadzki","date":". 01-01-2026, 03:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1094-eric-zawadzki","name":"Eric Zawadzki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;bas